WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.657 --> 00:00:05.740
MATTHEW SPALDING: Welcome.

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:08.970
Welcome to Hillside College's
Washington, DC, campus.

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:11.328
I'm Matthew Spalding, the
vice president for the college

00:00:11.328 --> 00:00:12.870
back here in
Washington, DC, and also

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:16.553
the dean of our new Van Andel
Graduate School of Government.

00:00:16.553 --> 00:00:18.720
And I'm here this evening,
we are here this evening,

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.590
with a great friend of mine
and a wonderful scholar,

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:23.750
Amity Shlaes.

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:28.950
Amity was previously at the
Wall Street Journal's editorial

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:31.560
board and published
a weekly column

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:36.150
and wrote a great book in 1999
called The Greedy Hand, where

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:37.020
I first met her.

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:40.152
We had her do something
for that book.

00:00:40.152 --> 00:00:41.610
She's written a
very important book

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:45.210
on the New Deal, The Forgotten
Man, A New History of the Great

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:48.840
Depression, of which there's
a graphic version, which

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:51.360
I have not seen, I have to say.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.760
And then a book which
is a personal favorite,

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.820
although those are
great works, which

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:00.300
came to my attention,
wonderfully titled

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:04.440
just Coolidge,
she wrote in 2013.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:07.230
And she's on the Calvin
Coolidge Presidential Foundation

00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:09.550
Board, which I'm a big fan of.

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:11.385
So we have a number
of things in common.

00:01:11.385 --> 00:01:15.150
But tonight, we're going to
talk about her new book, which

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:18.960
is The Great Society, A New
History, which is just out,

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:21.570
and we have copies of here
for all of you to buy.

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:25.140
And I have been reading it
and enjoying it immensely,

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:28.590
especially given the
connection between that book

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:32.348
and your previous
book on the New Deal.

00:01:32.348 --> 00:01:34.140
And I'm struck, as I've
always been struck,

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:37.770
about how so many the questions
that we deal with today--

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:44.130
health care, education,
poverty, standards of living--

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:47.130
these are issues that came
out of the Great Society.

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:50.250
But you seem to be saying
it's not that great.

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:51.950
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:54.720
And indeed, it might
have had some problems.

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:56.010
AMITY SHLAES: Might have.

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:57.677
MATTHEW SPALDING: And
so I want to start

00:01:57.677 --> 00:02:01.770
with some general
questions about the book,

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:03.267
but also about that period.

00:02:03.267 --> 00:02:05.100
Because it turns out
there's a lot of things

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.320
we can learn from studying
that period that might

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:09.990
be extremely applicable today.

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:17.640
So start us off by, if we're
looking back in history,

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:19.770
how should we look at
what is the Great Society

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:22.120
and how should we
perceive that as a thing?

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:23.212
What do we make of that?

00:02:23.212 --> 00:02:25.170
AMITY SHLAES: How should
we-- a great society--

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:27.000
I think you might
have seen this morning

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.600
that one of the presidential
candidates, Mike Bloomberg,

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:34.530
said it was time for
a war on poverty.

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:37.800
So that's a phrase
from the Great Society.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.385
I think you always
start with what--

00:02:40.385 --> 00:02:42.510
I would just stop and say
I'm very glad to be here,

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:45.810
and I'm very impressed with
Hillsdale, and a new graduate

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:48.540
school, and Dr. Spalding,
and the Van Andel Lecture

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:49.920
Hall and School.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:52.290
What an achievement this is.

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:54.570
A presence that
was not here before

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:59.700
is here, and very central
and very admirable.

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:01.690
So what do we learn in school--

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:02.940
though maybe not at Hillsdale.

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:05.190
But what do we generally
learn in school

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:07.840
about the Great
Society in the '60s?

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:13.020
Well, the '60s are
taught non-logically.

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:14.860
That's the first thing to say.

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:17.070
So when you hear about
the '60s, it usually

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:19.480
tries to replicate the
kaleidoscope, maybe

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:23.400
the drug-related experience.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:30.210
So there'll be loud music,
Woodstock, napalm, tragedy,

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:37.080
hippies, Nixon looking
bereaved and resentful.

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:40.530
And so it's almost hard
to get a logical picture.

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:43.650
Certainly, it's hard to get
a chronology of the period

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:45.180
from the way it's
taught, and that's

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:47.520
a separate problem to
the problems of earlier

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:52.080
periods where, for whatever
sins are contained in earlier

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:56.460
tellings of earlier periods,
they are usually chronological.

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:58.920
And man is chronological.

00:03:58.920 --> 00:03:59.730
We need that.

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:03.650
Thematic is very difficult
for us to retain or analyze.

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:05.040
It's sort of pre-analyzed.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:07.825
So that's the problem with the
history of the Great Society.

00:04:07.825 --> 00:04:09.450
The biggest problem
is not left/right--

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:12.120
[CLEARS THROAT] excuse me-- or
where you are in the Vietnam

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:13.870
War or when you switched.

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:18.510
It's that it's taught more
with emotion and kaleidoscope

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:20.490
than with logic, to me.

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:22.380
So I said, well,
what is this period?

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:26.070
In the 1930s, we
had the New Deal.

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:28.020
And what is Great Society?

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:32.350
It sounds as
ambitious as New Deal.

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:35.140
And it was, in fact, more
ambitious because the New

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:39.970
Deal took place in a time of
genuine economic emergency.

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:42.970
If one in four is unemployed,
that's a real problem,

00:04:42.970 --> 00:04:45.970
and people have to
come up with an answer.

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:50.860
And Franklin Roosevelt-- before
him, Herbert Hoover-- did.

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:52.780
And we may not like
the answer and have

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:57.460
had another that we would have
liked to have applied in 1932

00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:00.490
or '33, but there's
the unavoidable fact

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:03.140
of the national emergency.

00:05:03.140 --> 00:05:05.740
This is a different story
because we were actually doing

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:07.640
pretty well in the early '60s.

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:12.190
Everything seemed to be
getting better in every way.

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:14.690
The '50s were a
pretty good decade.

00:05:14.690 --> 00:05:18.118
Everything seemed to be
getting better in every way.

00:05:18.118 --> 00:05:19.660
And we'll come back
to this, Matthew,

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:22.905
but you know that poverty
went down dramatically,

00:05:22.905 --> 00:05:27.160
if you want to make war on
poverty, in the 1950s basically

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:29.410
because of economic growth.

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:33.610
So what's the thing you give a
poor person more than anything

00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:34.570
else that he wants?

00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:36.130
A job, right?

00:05:36.130 --> 00:05:40.390
And so the '50s turned out to be
a great decade with jobs, even

00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:42.830
sometimes with benefits.

00:05:42.830 --> 00:05:47.470
And we thought, oh, well,
given how strong we are

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:50.200
and that we're a
superpower and all,

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:55.920
let's just clean up the rest
of our little problems-- cure

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:58.590
poverty, as Franklin
Roosevelt said.

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:01.980
And I remember the writer,
Norman Podhoretz, said,

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:04.170
we all thought poverty
could be cured.

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:05.680
This was, just
after World War II,

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:07.680
for those who had fought
in World War II, a kind

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:10.170
of mopping-up action, domestic.

00:06:10.170 --> 00:06:15.330
Because if you can win Europe
or defeat Japan, of course

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:18.340
you can help poor people,
and all will be better.

00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:21.750
That was the very ambitious
attitude in the early '60s.

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:24.537
MATTHEW SPALDING: Yeah,
there's a great chart

00:06:24.537 --> 00:06:26.370
in the back of the book
showing the decline.

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:29.980
It's very small [INAUDIBLE],,
but showing the decline

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:31.800
of the rate of poverty.

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:34.110
But then it kind of
flattens out after that.

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:36.150
AMITY SHLAES:
That's right, yeah.

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:38.640
And I want to mention
here John Cogan of Hoover

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:41.453
who wrote a fantastic book
called The High Cost of--

00:06:41.453 --> 00:06:42.870
MATTHEW SPALDING:
Good intentions.

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:44.453
AMITY SHLAES: --Good
Intentions, yeah.

00:06:44.453 --> 00:06:47.160
Anyway, it's all data,
and it's all true.

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:50.760
So he also gets at this point.

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:54.990
We did not cure poverty even
though Franklin Roosevelt hoped

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:58.710
we might and Lyndon
Johnson swore we would.

00:06:58.710 --> 00:07:03.950
We may have brought
it down a bit at first

00:07:03.950 --> 00:07:05.500
when the Great Society started.

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:09.100
And you look at that chart, and
you see poverty going down sort

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:10.060
of as fast--

00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:12.850
and you can look at the points
underlying the PowerPoint

00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:13.750
and decide--

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:15.190
as it did in the 1950s.

00:07:15.190 --> 00:07:18.370
And then poverty, by
the official definition

00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:23.430
established by our government,
leveled off at 10%,

00:07:23.430 --> 00:07:24.900
a little more.

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:26.010
And it's still there.

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:29.070
So curing poverty didn't happen.

00:07:29.070 --> 00:07:31.680
That was the promise
of the Great Society,

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:33.743
and it didn't happen.

00:07:33.743 --> 00:07:34.660
That's the main thing.

00:07:34.660 --> 00:07:37.590
And I'm really happy to have
the Framers here instead

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:38.678
of a PowerPoint for you.

00:07:38.678 --> 00:07:41.855
MATTHEW SPALDING:
[LAUGHS] So you've

00:07:41.855 --> 00:07:43.980
already alluded to the
comparison between the Great

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:45.810
Society and the New Deal.

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:48.258
Let's broaden that
a bit and look

00:07:48.258 --> 00:07:49.800
at the various, what
we want to call,

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:52.230
waves of liberalism
in the 20th century.

00:07:52.230 --> 00:07:53.730
Because the other
one we have to add

00:07:53.730 --> 00:07:57.030
to that is the early
Progressive movement.

00:07:57.030 --> 00:08:01.800
And it strikes me that it might
be that the Great Society has

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:05.610
actually more similarities
to the early Progressives.

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:07.620
I mean, there was a
sense that somehow they

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:12.210
could fix things through
expertise and more government

00:08:12.210 --> 00:08:13.460
than--

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:15.330
where do you put
the Great Society

00:08:15.330 --> 00:08:20.310
in this larger motif of
what modern liberalism did

00:08:20.310 --> 00:08:21.240
over the 20th century?

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:22.740
AMITY SHLAES: Well,
we used to think

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:24.630
that Progressive social
workers worked where?

00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:26.900
In the states.

00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:29.570
The lady from the state of
Missouri or from the county

00:08:29.570 --> 00:08:33.169
came to your house and said
whatever she had to say.

00:08:33.169 --> 00:08:36.863
They might be funded by the
federal government in part,

00:08:36.863 --> 00:08:38.030
but it came from the states.

00:08:38.030 --> 00:08:40.880
So early Progressivism,
the kind that

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:45.140
inspired Theodore Roosevelt, was
in the state of Massachusetts,

00:08:45.140 --> 00:08:46.560
in the state of New York.

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:50.180
And that's where the
Progressives came from.

00:08:50.180 --> 00:08:55.220
We never imagined we would
be running the social work

00:08:55.220 --> 00:09:00.710
business from Washington
even in times of prosperity.

00:09:00.710 --> 00:09:02.570
Most of us didn't, anyhow.

00:09:02.570 --> 00:09:05.090
So what happened in the
early 1960s is we said,

00:09:05.090 --> 00:09:07.340
forget the states.

00:09:07.340 --> 00:09:09.050
The federal government
needs to do this.

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:10.740
Only the federal
government can do it.

00:09:10.740 --> 00:09:14.130
And the book opens actually
with Michael Harrington,

00:09:14.130 --> 00:09:16.460
who was a socialist.

00:09:16.460 --> 00:09:18.830
He didn't work for Moscow.

00:09:18.830 --> 00:09:20.330
What was wrong with
these socialists

00:09:20.330 --> 00:09:22.010
was not that they were traitors.

00:09:22.010 --> 00:09:25.850
It was that they were wrong
about their philosophy, right?

00:09:25.850 --> 00:09:29.240
A nice man, kind of
an early JD Vance.

00:09:29.240 --> 00:09:30.650
He focused on hillbillies.

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:31.790
They had a problem.

00:09:31.790 --> 00:09:33.020
Some of that was social.

00:09:33.020 --> 00:09:36.440
Let's figure that
out-- alcohol trouble,

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:39.590
you know-- and wrote a book
called The Other America.

00:09:39.590 --> 00:09:44.880
And he said, the
problem of America,

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:48.890
these deep-seated problems of
families and no food sometimes,

00:09:48.890 --> 00:09:51.590
or the wrong food,
or wrong attitudes,

00:09:51.590 --> 00:09:55.610
only the federal government
is capable of solving this.

00:09:55.610 --> 00:09:57.330
Only Washington.

00:09:57.330 --> 00:10:00.230
And there was some consensus
for that even beginning

00:10:00.230 --> 00:10:03.980
under Kennedy, although Kennedy
didn't get the legislation.

00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:05.480
And it only strengthened.

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:06.920
Only Washington can do this.

00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:09.030
And remember-- I'll
just say one last thing.

00:10:09.030 --> 00:10:11.530
A lot of the men who were
leading our country-- and they

00:10:11.530 --> 00:10:13.100
were mostly men-- were what?

00:10:13.100 --> 00:10:14.780
Veterans.

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:18.320
So most of the time--
this is before Vietnam--

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:23.120
they had seen the federal
government do something good,

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:24.080
win a war.

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:26.930
And they might have had a
benefit or one or the other.

00:10:26.930 --> 00:10:30.320
When they came home, they
might have had GI Bill.

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:33.200
They might have had
subsidy for housing

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:36.350
that made entry-- and
certainly education.

00:10:36.350 --> 00:10:39.790
And there are people in
this room probably who--

00:10:39.790 --> 00:10:42.855
it was the attitude of
Daniel Patrick Moynihan.

00:10:42.855 --> 00:10:45.230
I never had to pay for school
because the government took

00:10:45.230 --> 00:10:46.250
care of me.

00:10:46.250 --> 00:10:48.050
I trust the government.

00:10:48.050 --> 00:10:49.940
So they did trust
the government,

00:10:49.940 --> 00:10:51.950
the federal government,
at a very high level.

00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:53.742
MATTHEW SPALDING: So
does the Great Society

00:10:53.742 --> 00:10:55.250
grow out of the New Deal?

00:10:55.250 --> 00:10:57.140
Does it grow out of
this earlier root?

00:10:57.140 --> 00:10:58.370
Is it something different?

00:10:58.370 --> 00:10:59.810
Is there a shift in liberalism?

00:10:59.810 --> 00:11:01.060
AMITY SHLAES: It's just more--

00:11:01.060 --> 00:11:02.630
MATTHEW SPALDING:
What is your read?

00:11:02.630 --> 00:11:05.090
AMITY SHLAES: It's
just more ambitious.

00:11:05.090 --> 00:11:06.770
So it grows.

00:11:06.770 --> 00:11:09.320
I mean, if you look
for logic in it,

00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:13.860
you can find strands of logic
depending on the person, right?

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:17.120
But what it was was
a general consensus

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:18.900
that we had to do something.

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:22.010
And imagine we're
having a theater.

00:11:22.010 --> 00:11:24.380
In the background, the bombs
are going off all the time.

00:11:24.380 --> 00:11:26.420
Offstage-- that's
Vietnam, right?

00:11:26.420 --> 00:11:30.350
Offstage, the battle
rumbles, and the tension

00:11:30.350 --> 00:11:35.630
of sending 400,000, 500,000
people very suddenly over

00:11:35.630 --> 00:11:36.720
to Asia is there.

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:39.810
So this idea,
Vietnam is a strand.

00:11:39.810 --> 00:11:41.540
The Progressive
impulse is a strand.

00:11:41.540 --> 00:11:48.320
And another strand is Lyndon
Johnson's personal ambition.

00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:50.750
Lots of biographers--
[INAUDIBLE],, most recently--

00:11:50.750 --> 00:11:51.760
have covered that.

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:55.520
But here's a person who's
the child of the New Deal.

00:11:55.520 --> 00:11:59.060
He was a Roosevelt pleaser,
a very effective congressman.

00:11:59.060 --> 00:12:01.220
And it was said--
there's a cartoon.

00:12:01.220 --> 00:12:06.560
If you want something done
quick in the New Deal,

00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:08.540
you get Lyndon
Johnson, the young man

00:12:08.540 --> 00:12:11.090
who worked with youth
in the state of Texas,

00:12:11.090 --> 00:12:14.030
and actually had to tell the
farmers they should plow over

00:12:14.030 --> 00:12:17.390
their crops, and that
was economic logic.

00:12:17.390 --> 00:12:20.690
You plow over your crop,
and then you get money

00:12:20.690 --> 00:12:22.460
from the federal government.

00:12:22.460 --> 00:12:24.900
And the farmers, he had
to convince the farmers.

00:12:24.900 --> 00:12:26.900
And the farmers were kind
of dubious about that,

00:12:26.900 --> 00:12:29.540
and their mules were
even more dubious

00:12:29.540 --> 00:12:31.400
because the mules
in Texas are trained

00:12:31.400 --> 00:12:34.730
not to step on the little
fledgling plants, right?

00:12:34.730 --> 00:12:38.570
The whole experience of a
mule is not to step on plants.

00:12:38.570 --> 00:12:40.970
And here, they were
destroying something.

00:12:40.970 --> 00:12:46.830
So Lyndon Johnson did yeoman
service to Franklin Roosevelt

00:12:46.830 --> 00:12:52.700
on executing a sometimes
nonsensical New Deal.

00:12:52.700 --> 00:12:55.610
And he said, when I
grow up, I'm going

00:12:55.610 --> 00:12:58.550
to finish Franklin
Roosevelt's agenda,

00:12:58.550 --> 00:13:00.650
and I'm going to finish
Harry Truman's agenda.

00:13:00.650 --> 00:13:02.660
Harry Truman wanted
something like Medicare.

00:13:02.660 --> 00:13:04.340
He hadn't got it through.

00:13:04.340 --> 00:13:06.708
Johnson was deeply
proud, a proud man,

00:13:06.708 --> 00:13:08.000
that he could get that through.

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:10.400
But you want to
think he's ambitious,

00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:13.430
so he plays to his strengths,
a master of the Senate.

00:13:13.430 --> 00:13:16.740
And I don't even focus too
much on Johnson in the book,

00:13:16.740 --> 00:13:17.720
so we'll stop there.

00:13:17.720 --> 00:13:21.160
Because he's just
part of the story.

00:13:21.160 --> 00:13:24.955
But that's him,
ambition and glory.

00:13:24.955 --> 00:13:27.080
MATTHEW SPALDING: Right,
perhaps even being greater

00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:27.890
than the New Deal.

00:13:27.890 --> 00:13:28.280
AMITY SHLAES: Yeah.

00:13:28.280 --> 00:13:30.113
MATTHEW SPALDING: Because
one of your charts

00:13:30.113 --> 00:13:32.565
shows how the Great
Society programs actually

00:13:32.565 --> 00:13:34.940
are larger than the New Deal
programs at a certain point.

00:13:34.940 --> 00:13:35.900
AMITY SHLAES: Oh,
of course, yeah.

00:13:35.900 --> 00:13:37.610
And if you have a chart
which we have in the back,

00:13:37.610 --> 00:13:39.527
you will see that our
commitments to the Great

00:13:39.527 --> 00:13:42.890
Society cost more
than our commitments

00:13:42.890 --> 00:13:44.570
to New Deal programs today.

00:13:44.570 --> 00:13:46.490
That is, the papers
our lawmakers

00:13:46.490 --> 00:13:49.850
signed in the '60s committed
us to spending more

00:13:49.850 --> 00:13:51.860
today than even the New Deal.

00:13:51.860 --> 00:13:55.400
What's interesting is the Walter
Lippmann, the commentator,

00:13:55.400 --> 00:13:58.580
wrote a critique
of the New Deal.

00:13:58.580 --> 00:14:01.460
And it was called
a Good Society.

00:14:01.460 --> 00:14:04.550
And one reason Walter
Lippmann called it good

00:14:04.550 --> 00:14:08.150
was he was signaling we should
draw back from these Superman

00:14:08.150 --> 00:14:10.340
ambition projects.

00:14:10.340 --> 00:14:10.900
Great.

00:14:10.900 --> 00:14:11.970
But great is wrong.

00:14:11.970 --> 00:14:12.470
Good.

00:14:12.470 --> 00:14:16.180
But Johnson liked "great."

00:14:16.180 --> 00:14:19.060
He was very proud of the phrase.

00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:24.040
He introduced it at the
University of Michigan in 1964.

00:14:24.040 --> 00:14:27.150
And if not great, then not
at all, was his attitude.

00:14:27.150 --> 00:14:28.150
MATTHEW SPALDING: Right.

00:14:28.150 --> 00:14:30.430
I think the phrase "Great
Society" was actually

00:14:30.430 --> 00:14:31.870
used by John Dewey at one.

00:14:31.870 --> 00:14:33.717
It's actually an old
Progressive phrase--

00:14:33.717 --> 00:14:35.050
AMITY SHLAES: Yes, it was even--

00:14:35.050 --> 00:14:35.500
MATTHEW SPALDING: --in an essay.

00:14:35.500 --> 00:14:37.570
AMITY SHLAES: --used by the
Fabian Socialists in England.

00:14:37.570 --> 00:14:38.470
MATTHEW SPALDING:
Yeah, so it does

00:14:38.470 --> 00:14:39.340
actually have a history to it.

00:14:39.340 --> 00:14:41.423
AMITY SHLAES: And we will
have great-- in England,

00:14:41.423 --> 00:14:42.760
it also means big, right?

00:14:42.760 --> 00:14:44.780
It doesn't just mean high.

00:14:44.780 --> 00:14:48.790
So by "great society,"
actually in England,

00:14:48.790 --> 00:14:52.870
they meant a society that is
networked from town to town

00:14:52.870 --> 00:14:54.070
across the country.

00:14:54.070 --> 00:14:56.500
Because before the
railroad and the telegraph,

00:14:56.500 --> 00:14:58.300
towns were pretty isolated.

00:14:58.300 --> 00:15:01.330
And now we had a great
society, and the newspapers

00:15:01.330 --> 00:15:04.060
began to come on the
train in the morning,

00:15:04.060 --> 00:15:06.700
and we could hear what was going
on in London in practically

00:15:06.700 --> 00:15:07.690
real time.

00:15:07.690 --> 00:15:10.990
That was what the Fabian
Socialists imagined.

00:15:10.990 --> 00:15:12.970
But they also
imagined, right away,

00:15:12.970 --> 00:15:16.980
that if a society is
great, broad, high,

00:15:16.980 --> 00:15:19.170
it must have great planners.

00:15:19.170 --> 00:15:20.170
MATTHEW SPALDING: Right.

00:15:20.170 --> 00:15:21.630
AMITY SHLAES: You can see
that in the language too.

00:15:21.630 --> 00:15:22.547
MATTHEW SPALDING: Yes.

00:15:22.547 --> 00:15:25.710
AMITY SHLAES: And planning is
a huge part of Great Society,

00:15:25.710 --> 00:15:26.520
ours, in the '60s.

00:15:26.520 --> 00:15:28.020
MATTHEW SPALDING:
Right, that's what

00:15:28.020 --> 00:15:30.540
I mean, the sense of going
back to the early Progressives,

00:15:30.540 --> 00:15:31.808
planning and expertise.

00:15:31.808 --> 00:15:33.600
So the main elements
of the Great Society--

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:35.390
correct me if I'm wrong--

00:15:35.390 --> 00:15:43.120
in LBJ, his famous speech,
health care, health, education,

00:15:43.120 --> 00:15:43.830
the environment.

00:15:43.830 --> 00:15:45.653
Are those really the main areas?

00:15:45.653 --> 00:15:47.070
AMITY SHLAES: Yeah,
he had cities.

00:15:47.070 --> 00:15:50.250
He classed them cities,
country, classroom.

00:15:50.250 --> 00:15:51.960
And all those things are--

00:15:51.960 --> 00:15:54.075
MATTHEW SPALDING:
Why those, and what

00:15:54.075 --> 00:15:55.200
was their thinking on them?

00:15:55.200 --> 00:15:57.720
AMITY SHLAES: Well, we did
need some environmental law

00:15:57.720 --> 00:15:59.700
or environmental habit.

00:15:59.700 --> 00:16:01.840
That was clear.

00:16:01.840 --> 00:16:06.300
This was the era when you
couldn't drive through Gary

00:16:06.300 --> 00:16:08.550
because it smelled too bad.

00:16:08.550 --> 00:16:12.200
We did, but you had to
roll up the window, right?

00:16:15.840 --> 00:16:17.630
LBJ was very
concerned about-- he

00:16:17.630 --> 00:16:19.080
was very much of a car person.

00:16:19.080 --> 00:16:21.590
You don't see him walking a lot.

00:16:21.590 --> 00:16:24.140
And I think America would
be different if presidents

00:16:24.140 --> 00:16:28.320
could walk down the street
because he was a vehicular

00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:28.820
president.

00:16:28.820 --> 00:16:31.820
He had an amphibious car.

00:16:31.820 --> 00:16:34.370
So he liked to
beautify highways.

00:16:34.370 --> 00:16:37.010
Lady Bird did along with
beginning in the environment.

00:16:37.010 --> 00:16:39.320
Of course, Nixon did
more, and I include

00:16:39.320 --> 00:16:40.765
Nixon in the Great Society.

00:16:40.765 --> 00:16:42.140
One of the revisions
of this book

00:16:42.140 --> 00:16:45.890
is that Nixon is really
just a follow-on to Johnson.

00:16:45.890 --> 00:16:49.730
The classroom, Johnson
was a school teacher,

00:16:49.730 --> 00:16:51.560
so that appealed to him.

00:16:51.560 --> 00:16:54.590
And he believed, rightly, I
would say-- and you would too,

00:16:54.590 --> 00:16:56.960
probably-- that
education is much

00:16:56.960 --> 00:17:00.080
of the answer to the
ills of poor families.

00:17:00.080 --> 00:17:04.250
If they know more, they
can earn more money.

00:17:04.250 --> 00:17:05.130
And--

00:17:05.130 --> 00:17:07.040
MATTHEW SPALDING:
OK, so one could

00:17:07.040 --> 00:17:09.990
say that's a reasonable argument
for those three areas of--

00:17:09.990 --> 00:17:10.839
AMITY SHLAES: Right.

00:17:10.839 --> 00:17:12.530
MATTHEW SPALDING: --involvement.

00:17:12.530 --> 00:17:15.440
How did it become
the Great Society?

00:17:15.440 --> 00:17:17.569
Was this good
intentions gone bad?

00:17:17.569 --> 00:17:21.290
Was this the fatal
conceit of planning?

00:17:21.290 --> 00:17:25.160
Was this just great ambition
to get the federal government

00:17:25.160 --> 00:17:25.760
involved?

00:17:25.760 --> 00:17:28.290
Was it to build--

00:17:28.290 --> 00:17:28.790
what's the--

00:17:28.790 --> 00:17:30.860
AMITY SHLAES: Well,
it was a power drive.

00:17:30.860 --> 00:17:32.418
We want to stay in
power, so we want

00:17:32.418 --> 00:17:33.960
to do more and more
impressive things

00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:35.600
so people will re-elect us.

00:17:35.600 --> 00:17:40.130
That's the way-- the book ends
with Nixon seeking re-election.

00:17:40.130 --> 00:17:43.340
But I'm not sure
what you're asking.

00:17:43.340 --> 00:17:45.770
What I do say in the book,
what I show in the book,

00:17:45.770 --> 00:17:49.070
is first they tried
the war on poverty,

00:17:49.070 --> 00:17:53.480
and they had weird
results with that.

00:17:53.480 --> 00:17:56.510
The Poverty Law, the
Economic Opportunity Act,

00:17:56.510 --> 00:18:00.770
which created the Poverty
Office of Sargent Shriver,

00:18:00.770 --> 00:18:03.830
seemed a fairly harmless
law, but actually did

00:18:03.830 --> 00:18:05.510
quite a bit of harm.

00:18:05.510 --> 00:18:06.620
What am I thinking of?

00:18:06.620 --> 00:18:09.920
Many in this room are concerned
about federalism, right?

00:18:09.920 --> 00:18:12.380
What is the state in
charge of, and what

00:18:12.380 --> 00:18:14.870
is a town in charge of?

00:18:14.870 --> 00:18:18.410
We had poverty in
American cities.

00:18:18.410 --> 00:18:21.690
The mayors all had poverty.

00:18:21.690 --> 00:18:25.770
People-- maybe they called
them assistance, not poverty,

00:18:25.770 --> 00:18:27.870
but they had poverty people.

00:18:27.870 --> 00:18:30.120
Many of the mayors
were Democrats.

00:18:30.120 --> 00:18:33.830
They helped Lyndon Johnson
get elected in 1964.

00:18:33.830 --> 00:18:37.530
I'm thinking here
of Richard J. Daly.

00:18:37.530 --> 00:18:40.920
And so when Johnson
declared war on poverty,

00:18:40.920 --> 00:18:43.620
Richard J. Daly put
all his poverty ideas

00:18:43.620 --> 00:18:46.960
in a box, a big box,
mailed it to Washington.

00:18:46.960 --> 00:18:49.560
Here's what I need money
for that you owe me

00:18:49.560 --> 00:18:51.930
because of what I gave you.

00:18:51.930 --> 00:18:56.580
And that is not what came out of
the poverty czar office because

00:18:56.580 --> 00:19:00.570
of the infatuation with
reform and sometimes

00:19:00.570 --> 00:19:02.940
Social Democratic,
or we would sometimes

00:19:02.940 --> 00:19:04.200
call them socialist ideas.

00:19:04.200 --> 00:19:09.360
What came out of that office
was Sargent Shriver, a nice man,

00:19:09.360 --> 00:19:15.480
brother-in-law of the late
president, kind of noble,

00:19:15.480 --> 00:19:20.190
hard to dislike, veteran,
created the Peace Corps,

00:19:20.190 --> 00:19:25.602
running this, sent his own
poverty people to the towns.

00:19:25.602 --> 00:19:27.060
And I'm thinking
some of the people

00:19:27.060 --> 00:19:28.477
in the room may
have known of that

00:19:28.477 --> 00:19:30.210
or been part-- his
own poverty people.

00:19:30.210 --> 00:19:35.490
And those poverty people
believed in community activism,

00:19:35.490 --> 00:19:38.370
by which they meant,
let's go to the town hall

00:19:38.370 --> 00:19:42.660
and protest and try to
get whatever we need here.

00:19:42.660 --> 00:19:44.610
We thought it would be
jobs, but it ended up

00:19:44.610 --> 00:19:47.760
being housing,
landlords, and so on.

00:19:47.760 --> 00:19:49.680
And the mayors could
not believe it.

00:19:49.680 --> 00:19:53.040
They had elected Lyndon
Johnson, and now Johnson's very

00:19:53.040 --> 00:19:57.270
own people were bothering
them in their mayor office,

00:19:57.270 --> 00:19:59.760
disturbing their day,
and not helping them

00:19:59.760 --> 00:20:01.913
with their problem of poverty.

00:20:01.913 --> 00:20:03.330
And in the book,
I tell this story

00:20:03.330 --> 00:20:06.600
of Sam Yorty, the mayor
of Los Angeles, who

00:20:06.600 --> 00:20:10.890
said, there will be riots in
Los Angeles unless you help me,

00:20:10.890 --> 00:20:14.130
President Johnson, basically,
with my poverty programs.

00:20:14.130 --> 00:20:17.760
And he got into a
jurisdictional squabble

00:20:17.760 --> 00:20:20.490
with the Poverty Office,
the mayor of Los Angeles

00:20:20.490 --> 00:20:23.730
versus Sargent Shriver,
[INAUDIBLE] all spring.

00:20:23.730 --> 00:20:25.810
And the money that was
promised didn't come.

00:20:25.810 --> 00:20:27.130
What's worse than no money?

00:20:27.130 --> 00:20:29.763
Money that was promised
and doesn't come.

00:20:29.763 --> 00:20:31.680
What's worse than money
that's never promised?

00:20:31.680 --> 00:20:33.330
Money that was promised.

00:20:33.330 --> 00:20:36.310
And that failure of Yorty--

00:20:36.310 --> 00:20:38.700
and it was his
failure too-- to bring

00:20:38.700 --> 00:20:42.360
the money he said would be
there to create hundreds

00:20:42.360 --> 00:20:45.030
and hundreds of jobs
for the youth of Watts

00:20:45.030 --> 00:20:47.412
contributed to the
rage in Watts, along

00:20:47.412 --> 00:20:48.870
with all the other
factors that are

00:20:48.870 --> 00:20:52.680
more famous, such as the rough
police department and so on.

00:20:52.680 --> 00:20:55.950
So sometimes when you have
multiple jurisdiction,

00:20:55.950 --> 00:21:01.320
all you have is jurisdictional
lacunae and chaos, right?

00:21:01.320 --> 00:21:03.270
And that often happened.

00:21:03.270 --> 00:21:05.160
The other mayors
also complained.

00:21:05.160 --> 00:21:11.040
Daly said, does the president
know he's sending M-O-N-E-Y

00:21:11.040 --> 00:21:11.580
to--

00:21:11.580 --> 00:21:13.590
I can't remember what
he put, but subversive,

00:21:13.590 --> 00:21:15.330
some word like subversives.

00:21:15.330 --> 00:21:16.970
Does he know that?

00:21:16.970 --> 00:21:21.120
Daly couldn't believe it, that
Johnson would be so naive.

00:21:21.120 --> 00:21:23.220
And I think Johnson
himself was not

00:21:23.220 --> 00:21:25.260
really aware of what
Shriver was doing.

00:21:25.260 --> 00:21:28.800
And Shriver just wanted
to get things done fast,

00:21:28.800 --> 00:21:30.630
and Johnson was pressuring him.

00:21:30.630 --> 00:21:32.340
So this was a great--

00:21:32.340 --> 00:21:35.220
Shriver is one of
the fools of my book

00:21:35.220 --> 00:21:38.790
because he was a nice
man who did a naive thing

00:21:38.790 --> 00:21:40.408
and paid for it terribly.

00:21:40.408 --> 00:21:41.700
MATTHEW SPALDING: Right, right.

00:21:41.700 --> 00:21:45.103
So let's try to draw out
some broader lessons here,

00:21:45.103 --> 00:21:46.770
and then we'll open
it up for questions.

00:21:46.770 --> 00:21:47.728
AMITY SHLAES: Oh, sure.

00:21:47.728 --> 00:21:50.440
MATTHEW SPALDING: Because
there's a lot in your book

00:21:50.440 --> 00:21:53.250
which I found
fascinating about how

00:21:53.250 --> 00:22:01.260
there was a push for an idea,
perhaps because of idealism,

00:22:01.260 --> 00:22:03.640
perhaps because of politics--

00:22:03.640 --> 00:22:05.850
but then there's often
a compromise or a push

00:22:05.850 --> 00:22:10.350
back by people like Wilbur Mills
or Daniel Patrick Moynihan.

00:22:10.350 --> 00:22:13.980
But they got taken
on that because it

00:22:13.980 --> 00:22:15.960
took on a life of its own.

00:22:15.960 --> 00:22:19.583
Is the lesson here, beware
of idealism and government

00:22:19.583 --> 00:22:20.250
coming together?

00:22:20.250 --> 00:22:22.590
Is it unintended consequences?

00:22:22.590 --> 00:22:26.890
Is it, don't compromise
with idealists and zealots?

00:22:26.890 --> 00:22:28.568
I mean, what's the larger--

00:22:28.568 --> 00:22:30.360
AMITY SHLAES: Beware
of marginal increases,

00:22:30.360 --> 00:22:31.927
and we know that
from government.

00:22:31.927 --> 00:22:32.510
Wilbur Mills--

00:22:32.510 --> 00:22:33.290
MATTHEW SPALDING:
Some of these were--

00:22:33.290 --> 00:22:35.290
because you were pointing
out that-- so Medicare

00:22:35.290 --> 00:22:36.360
was an add-on.

00:22:36.360 --> 00:22:39.090
It was going to be a small
add-on program that was agreed

00:22:39.090 --> 00:22:41.367
to, but then it became--

00:22:41.367 --> 00:22:42.950
AMITY SHLAES: Yeah,
and Wilbur Mills--

00:22:42.950 --> 00:22:44.240
MATTHEW SPALDING: What do we
learn from this for today?

00:22:44.240 --> 00:22:44.990
Because this is
what we're debating.

00:22:44.990 --> 00:22:46.070
AMITY SHLAES: Wilbur Mills was--

00:22:46.070 --> 00:22:46.295
MATTHEW SPALDING: It's
the kind of debates

00:22:46.295 --> 00:22:48.200
we're having today
about what we should do.

00:22:48.200 --> 00:22:49.575
AMITY SHLAES: He
was a chairman--

00:22:49.575 --> 00:22:51.520
he was known as the
Powerful Wilbur Mills--

00:22:51.520 --> 00:22:54.600
I think, until I was 10, I
thought his name was Powerful--

00:22:54.600 --> 00:22:57.440
because the influence of the
Ways and Means Committee.

00:22:57.440 --> 00:23:02.150
And Wilbur Mills was a
government expander, but also

00:23:02.150 --> 00:23:03.380
a pragmatist.

00:23:03.380 --> 00:23:06.020
And he went along with
Medicare and kind of liked it

00:23:06.020 --> 00:23:08.060
because of a lot of
other trades he had

00:23:08.060 --> 00:23:09.830
going in his complex business.

00:23:09.830 --> 00:23:13.740
And then Medicare cost a lot
more than he thought it would,

00:23:13.740 --> 00:23:14.960
and he was embarrassed.

00:23:14.960 --> 00:23:17.030
He had told his
constituents at home

00:23:17.030 --> 00:23:19.580
it wouldn't cost this much.

00:23:19.580 --> 00:23:22.640
And he became wise.

00:23:22.640 --> 00:23:24.170
Everett Dirksen is the same way.

00:23:24.170 --> 00:23:26.060
Everett Dirksen
supported Johnson

00:23:26.060 --> 00:23:28.460
on civil rights, which was
very important to the end

00:23:28.460 --> 00:23:34.040
of the filibuster of laws
that are very important that

00:23:34.040 --> 00:23:35.850
are also in this period.

00:23:35.850 --> 00:23:38.240
But when he came
to some labor laws,

00:23:38.240 --> 00:23:40.880
Everett Dirksen said,
uh-uh, count me out.

00:23:40.880 --> 00:23:44.810
This is too much support
of organized labor.

00:23:44.810 --> 00:23:51.260
So I think if you would pick a
philosopher, it would be Hayek.

00:23:51.260 --> 00:23:52.633
And why Hayek?

00:23:52.633 --> 00:23:54.800
If you haven't heard of him
or don't know about him,

00:23:54.800 --> 00:23:58.250
Hayek spoke of a thing
called the knowledge problem.

00:23:58.250 --> 00:24:00.260
And basically what
it says is government

00:24:00.260 --> 00:24:04.040
is insulated from knowledge
by the political process.

00:24:04.040 --> 00:24:07.670
You only get feedback in an
election every four years,

00:24:07.670 --> 00:24:11.000
maybe every two years,
every six years, right?

00:24:11.000 --> 00:24:13.580
And you only talk to your
friends in government.

00:24:13.580 --> 00:24:17.370
Whereas the small business
is in the market every day.

00:24:17.370 --> 00:24:20.000
And by night, it knows
whether it did well

00:24:20.000 --> 00:24:23.470
with its product that day and
perhaps even how to adjust it.

00:24:23.470 --> 00:24:26.090
So the government
was so insulated

00:24:26.090 --> 00:24:28.730
from reality, most of the
time, it couldn't really

00:24:28.730 --> 00:24:32.990
figure out what it was doing
or what was wrong about that.

00:24:32.990 --> 00:24:36.890
And Hayek, of course,
also has Road to Serfdom.

00:24:36.890 --> 00:24:39.350
And what Road to Serfdom
says is we're not

00:24:39.350 --> 00:24:43.040
living in Zeno's paradox.

00:24:43.040 --> 00:24:45.560
You actually do get
to serfdom even if you

00:24:45.560 --> 00:24:48.710
take little steps, right?

00:24:48.710 --> 00:24:51.600
It's not like, oh,
we'll never get there.

00:24:51.600 --> 00:24:54.050
You actually get to serfdom
because entrenched interest

00:24:54.050 --> 00:24:56.220
groups-- in this case,
the silent majority,

00:24:56.220 --> 00:24:58.610
the forgotten men-- once
they have a benefit,

00:24:58.610 --> 00:25:02.310
as [INAUDIBLE] said, they
don't want to let it go.

00:25:02.310 --> 00:25:04.410
And when there are
enough entrenched groups,

00:25:04.410 --> 00:25:07.360
it's very hard to reform.

00:25:07.360 --> 00:25:09.693
And I feel even silly
saying this in Washington,

00:25:09.693 --> 00:25:11.110
where you know
this better than I.

00:25:11.110 --> 00:25:14.730
MATTHEW SPALDING: [LAUGHS] Let's
open up for some questions.

00:25:14.730 --> 00:25:16.730
I know that there's some
microphones, I believe.

00:25:18.938 --> 00:25:20.230
Fellow right there in the back.

00:25:25.920 --> 00:25:28.390
AUDIENCE: Thank you very
much for coming to speak.

00:25:28.390 --> 00:25:30.560
One of the things--

00:25:30.560 --> 00:25:34.960
maybe a little less now, but
certainly 10 or more years ago,

00:25:34.960 --> 00:25:37.210
folks who were alive
either who were

00:25:37.210 --> 00:25:39.540
of the so-called
silent generation

00:25:39.540 --> 00:25:44.790
or of the very older so-called
baby boomer generation,

00:25:44.790 --> 00:25:47.940
will often say, if
Vietnam hadn't happened,

00:25:47.940 --> 00:25:49.560
the Great Society
would have worked.

00:25:49.560 --> 00:25:50.820
It would have been tremendous.

00:25:50.820 --> 00:25:54.120
I have known myself people
alive from that time period

00:25:54.120 --> 00:25:57.430
or activists or professors
who've told me that and said,

00:25:57.430 --> 00:26:00.215
if it had just been fully funded
and Vietnam hadn't happened,

00:26:00.215 --> 00:26:01.590
everything would
have been great.

00:26:01.590 --> 00:26:04.440
I am skeptical of that, but I'm
wondering how you particularly

00:26:04.440 --> 00:26:05.400
answer that question.

00:26:05.400 --> 00:26:07.067
AMITY SHLAES: Well,
I would refer to you

00:26:07.067 --> 00:26:08.480
the chart on page 440--

00:26:08.480 --> 00:26:09.150
or sorry.

00:26:09.150 --> 00:26:10.280
[LAUGHTER]

00:26:10.280 --> 00:26:11.700
Or maybe 439.

00:26:11.700 --> 00:26:12.780
That's the answer.

00:26:12.780 --> 00:26:15.960
What people began to
realize, that Vietnam

00:26:15.960 --> 00:26:19.140
is the great excuse story.

00:26:19.140 --> 00:26:23.880
And Vietnam did help
to bring about some

00:26:23.880 --> 00:26:25.820
of the more expansive
bits of legislation.

00:26:25.820 --> 00:26:27.570
You could say the Great
Society wouldn't--

00:26:27.570 --> 00:26:29.330
MATTHEW SPALDING: Are you
talking about the butter price

00:26:29.330 --> 00:26:30.220
here, the guns chart?

00:26:30.220 --> 00:26:30.675
AMITY SHLAES: Yeah.

00:26:30.675 --> 00:26:31.680
MATTHEW SPALDING: Page 435.

00:26:31.680 --> 00:26:32.638
AMITY SHLAES: Page 435.

00:26:32.638 --> 00:26:35.670
But what I'm saying is
maybe the Great Society

00:26:35.670 --> 00:26:38.010
would have been just the
Good Society without Vietnam

00:26:38.010 --> 00:26:40.302
because you have to have
something really good at home.

00:26:40.302 --> 00:26:42.930
That's the opposite to what
people are arguing with you.

00:26:42.930 --> 00:26:47.460
And second, even in the
'60s, by the late '60s,

00:26:47.460 --> 00:26:51.990
people saw that the butter at
home, the domestic programs,

00:26:51.990 --> 00:26:55.590
were going to cost
more than the guns.

00:26:55.590 --> 00:26:57.000
That line was crossed.

00:26:57.000 --> 00:27:00.900
I believe it's in
early '70s, '71.

00:27:00.900 --> 00:27:02.610
And even today, we
have this fallacy

00:27:02.610 --> 00:27:03.973
that defense costs so much.

00:27:03.973 --> 00:27:05.640
When you look at the
whole budget, which

00:27:05.640 --> 00:27:08.850
includes mandatory spending,
defense does not cost so much.

00:27:08.850 --> 00:27:12.210
It costs a relatively
small share of our economy

00:27:12.210 --> 00:27:15.340
compared to history,
period of the Korean War.

00:27:15.340 --> 00:27:18.330
So this argument isn't reality.

00:27:18.330 --> 00:27:22.680
And the reason, for
example, that we

00:27:22.680 --> 00:27:27.240
had the fiscal crisis of
1971 and the stagflation

00:27:27.240 --> 00:27:33.420
and high interest rates of the
'70s is not mostly Vietnam.

00:27:33.420 --> 00:27:35.450
It's the Great Society.

00:27:35.450 --> 00:27:39.810
And that's very-- foreigners
looked at the US and said,

00:27:39.810 --> 00:27:42.360
it's going kind of
Social Democratic.

00:27:42.360 --> 00:27:48.590
That's like us, which means its
growth advantage may be eroded.

00:27:48.590 --> 00:27:51.300
Its growth advantage-- the
US had a growth advantage.

00:27:51.300 --> 00:27:54.060
It was a freer
market than Europe.

00:27:54.060 --> 00:27:57.870
That growth advantage, that
gap between Scandinavia

00:27:57.870 --> 00:28:01.620
and Michigan, is narrowing
in terms of social cost.

00:28:01.620 --> 00:28:05.560
And therefore, the US
might not be so strong.

00:28:05.560 --> 00:28:08.940
So there are plenty
of pieces of data that

00:28:08.940 --> 00:28:10.970
refute your friend's argument.

00:28:14.309 --> 00:28:16.700
MATTHEW SPALDING: Yeah?

00:28:16.700 --> 00:28:19.560
AUDIENCE: Yes, Ms.
Shlaes, isn't it the case

00:28:19.560 --> 00:28:22.430
the reason the Utopianism
of the Great Society

00:28:22.430 --> 00:28:24.860
was almost unchallenged
was because there

00:28:24.860 --> 00:28:27.620
was a lack of faith
in civil society

00:28:27.620 --> 00:28:30.800
and especially even in
the Republican Party

00:28:30.800 --> 00:28:33.110
after Dwight Eisenhower
took the party over

00:28:33.110 --> 00:28:36.410
and Robert Taft died
of cancer in 1953?

00:28:36.410 --> 00:28:37.520
And in academe too.

00:28:37.520 --> 00:28:40.520
I mean, there were just
a few marginal people.

00:28:40.520 --> 00:28:43.730
In 1953, Robert Nesbitt
and Russell Kirk each

00:28:43.730 --> 00:28:45.760
published classics The
Quest for Community

00:28:45.760 --> 00:28:48.260
and the Conservative Tradition,
but they were considered

00:28:48.260 --> 00:28:49.940
wackos in outside society.

00:28:49.940 --> 00:28:51.690
AMITY SHLAES: They
were considered wackos.

00:28:51.690 --> 00:28:54.250
AUDIENCE: The Austrian
economist too.

00:28:54.250 --> 00:28:57.360
And the voter registration
was 2 and 1/2 Democratic

00:28:57.360 --> 00:28:58.680
to Republicans.

00:28:58.680 --> 00:29:00.595
Isn't this the
reason why Johnson--

00:29:00.595 --> 00:29:04.080
and Johnson was a master of
the legislative process--

00:29:04.080 --> 00:29:08.580
that the chance to commit the
Great Society even occurred?

00:29:08.580 --> 00:29:10.840
AMITY SHLAES: That's a
very important question.

00:29:10.840 --> 00:29:13.830
I used to have a debate with
Jonathan Alter of Newsweek.

00:29:13.830 --> 00:29:18.140
And he would say, before the New
Deal, Amity, there was nothing.

00:29:18.140 --> 00:29:20.640
And before the New Deal,
there was something.

00:29:20.640 --> 00:29:21.660
It was just local.

00:29:21.660 --> 00:29:24.420
It was the Italian
American Burial Society,

00:29:24.420 --> 00:29:30.240
or it was the county of x,
y, z and its little hospital

00:29:30.240 --> 00:29:31.320
led by--

00:29:31.320 --> 00:29:32.460
there was a--

00:29:32.460 --> 00:29:34.080
David [INAUDIBLE]
has a beautiful book

00:29:34.080 --> 00:29:36.720
about Tocqueville in America
which I always give to kids.

00:29:36.720 --> 00:29:39.720
And all the little weird
institutions, Mooseheart,

00:29:39.720 --> 00:29:41.908
the orphanage, very--

00:29:41.908 --> 00:29:42.825
AUDIENCE: Still there.

00:29:42.825 --> 00:29:43.110
Still there.

00:29:43.110 --> 00:29:43.520
AMITY SHLAES: Right?

00:29:43.520 --> 00:29:44.520
Yeah, still there.

00:29:44.520 --> 00:29:50.030
So Yeah, there was a contempt
for Tocqueville America.

00:29:50.030 --> 00:29:52.410
It seemed quaint and old.

00:29:52.410 --> 00:29:56.790
And it was a terrible contempt
because Tocqueville still

00:29:56.790 --> 00:29:59.250
makes America the little town.

00:29:59.250 --> 00:30:02.970
I have a chapter on the
fragility of the municipality

00:30:02.970 --> 00:30:05.100
where Tocqueville writes
about the fragility

00:30:05.100 --> 00:30:06.660
of the municipality can be--

00:30:06.660 --> 00:30:09.000
it's like a consumptive patient.

00:30:09.000 --> 00:30:11.430
It could die any time,
that municipality,

00:30:11.430 --> 00:30:14.400
because the big state, by
which Tocqueville probably

00:30:14.400 --> 00:30:17.100
meant the county or the state--

00:30:17.100 --> 00:30:21.270
that is, state of Illinois,
Springfield, Albany--

00:30:21.270 --> 00:30:25.680
might step on it
or pull the cord.

00:30:25.680 --> 00:30:29.430
So when you want to think about
Tocqueville's America in this

00:30:29.430 --> 00:30:32.430
period, I have a long
description of the housing

00:30:32.430 --> 00:30:36.330
program at Pruitt-Igoe in
St. Louis and what they did

00:30:36.330 --> 00:30:39.000
to the Tocqueville of
downtown St. Louis,

00:30:39.000 --> 00:30:42.840
which very proud town-- maybe
some of you are from there--

00:30:42.840 --> 00:30:47.850
with very local, strong, "let's
support each other" tradition

00:30:47.850 --> 00:30:52.030
just wrecked by urban
renewal and bulldozers.

00:30:52.030 --> 00:30:55.140
And I will just
say one last thing.

00:30:55.140 --> 00:30:57.810
For the middle class, we
had something of Tocqueville

00:30:57.810 --> 00:31:00.220
in our housing funding
because we said,

00:31:00.220 --> 00:31:04.860
we'll give middle-class
people subsidized mortgages

00:31:04.860 --> 00:31:08.050
so they can settle in little
communities, the new suburbs,

00:31:08.050 --> 00:31:08.550
right?

00:31:08.550 --> 00:31:12.000
And then they can have
a cul-de-sac and a car,

00:31:12.000 --> 00:31:14.430
and their children
can play there.

00:31:14.430 --> 00:31:15.867
And they'll build
a new community,

00:31:15.867 --> 00:31:17.700
and we'll fund that,
the federal government.

00:31:17.700 --> 00:31:19.800
That's also Levittown.

00:31:19.800 --> 00:31:21.390
That's for middle-class people.

00:31:21.390 --> 00:31:24.840
For poor people, they
can have Karl Marx,

00:31:24.840 --> 00:31:27.360
and we'll build big, tall,
really ugly buildings

00:31:27.360 --> 00:31:28.740
in the international
style, which

00:31:28.740 --> 00:31:30.450
we kind of admire
but tends to make

00:31:30.450 --> 00:31:32.470
people feel sad and lonely.

00:31:32.470 --> 00:31:36.390
And we'll send them
up in elevators.

00:31:36.390 --> 00:31:39.330
And in the halls, they'll be
beat up by wayward children,

00:31:39.330 --> 00:31:42.780
and there'll be no commerce
at all around them.

00:31:42.780 --> 00:31:44.970
And certainly, then let's
bulldoze the churches

00:31:44.970 --> 00:31:46.380
around the edge.

00:31:46.380 --> 00:31:48.418
Because we're bulldozing
whole communities,

00:31:48.418 --> 00:31:49.710
so most of the churches go too.

00:31:49.710 --> 00:31:52.050
And all the other
Tocqueville institutions

00:31:52.050 --> 00:31:54.690
that happen to have
been there around where

00:31:54.690 --> 00:31:56.497
the new tall buildings go.

00:31:56.497 --> 00:31:58.080
And at the end of
the book, I describe

00:31:58.080 --> 00:32:02.970
how, after all the successive
waves of Progressivism

00:32:02.970 --> 00:32:07.380
and non-Tocquevillism at
St. Louis' Pruitt-Igoe,

00:32:07.380 --> 00:32:12.200
they had to, as in
Chicago, blow it up.

00:32:12.200 --> 00:32:15.980
MATTHEW SPALDING: But
wasn't one of the--

00:32:15.980 --> 00:32:19.450
to go back to an earlier point
you made, there's a certain--

00:32:19.450 --> 00:32:22.260
politics is never
about one or the other

00:32:22.260 --> 00:32:23.410
in those absolute ways.

00:32:23.410 --> 00:32:25.140
It's very rarely that
kind of question.

00:32:25.140 --> 00:32:27.730
One of the things the Great
Society took advantage of

00:32:27.730 --> 00:32:30.580
was a natural
American desire to try

00:32:30.580 --> 00:32:34.750
to solve some of these
problems, poverty,

00:32:34.750 --> 00:32:36.760
to allow for growth
and expansion

00:32:36.760 --> 00:32:39.100
and a national economy, right?

00:32:39.100 --> 00:32:43.870
So they drove right
through that because there

00:32:43.870 --> 00:32:48.580
was a-- as the nation grew
and the economy grew and it

00:32:48.580 --> 00:32:52.060
became larger, there
was a need to solve

00:32:52.060 --> 00:32:53.810
some of these problems.

00:32:53.810 --> 00:32:55.450
They just thought
they could perfect it

00:32:55.450 --> 00:32:58.143
by using Washington
programs to do so.

00:32:58.143 --> 00:32:59.560
AMITY SHLAES: When
I was writing--

00:32:59.560 --> 00:33:00.060
yeah.

00:33:00.060 --> 00:33:01.600
So we want a great society.

00:33:01.600 --> 00:33:02.460
We're ambitious.

00:33:02.460 --> 00:33:03.490
It's 1960.

00:33:03.490 --> 00:33:07.720
We're teenagers, and we don't
remember World War II, right?

00:33:07.720 --> 00:33:10.360
The boomers were like
the woke students

00:33:10.360 --> 00:33:12.760
now who say, hey, boomer, right?

00:33:12.760 --> 00:33:17.800
Boomers were the generational
divide, the rebellion.

00:33:17.800 --> 00:33:21.220
And they said, we want
to do something great.

00:33:21.220 --> 00:33:24.330
The only question
is, by what means?

00:33:24.330 --> 00:33:27.650
Through the public sector,
or the private sector?

00:33:27.650 --> 00:33:30.340
Well, we opted for
the public sector.

00:33:30.340 --> 00:33:31.660
And what is the private sector?

00:33:31.660 --> 00:33:34.280
It's the milk cow that funds it.

00:33:34.280 --> 00:33:35.480
And you know what?

00:33:35.480 --> 00:33:37.720
If you leave the private
sector alone enough,

00:33:37.720 --> 00:33:40.450
it usually can fund
a lot of whatever

00:33:40.450 --> 00:33:43.250
you want to do with your
Great Society vision.

00:33:43.250 --> 00:33:45.220
So in the book when
I was writing it,

00:33:45.220 --> 00:33:48.400
I decided to tell the
story of three companies--

00:33:48.400 --> 00:33:55.120
General Electric; Toyota, which
intervenes suddenly in Detroit;

00:33:55.120 --> 00:33:59.500
and the company
that became Intel.

00:33:59.500 --> 00:34:02.420
And these companies
did social things.

00:34:02.420 --> 00:34:03.670
Number one, they created jobs.

00:34:03.670 --> 00:34:08.270
Number two, in Fairchild, which
is the predecessor to Intel,

00:34:08.270 --> 00:34:11.469
for example, at a factory on
a Native American reservation,

00:34:11.469 --> 00:34:14.110
it became the greatest
private sector

00:34:14.110 --> 00:34:16.540
employer of Native Americans.

00:34:16.540 --> 00:34:19.600
Because this particular
Native American group

00:34:19.600 --> 00:34:22.420
worked with their hands,
did a lot of handiwork,

00:34:22.420 --> 00:34:27.460
like weaving, basically, or
what we would call needlepoint,

00:34:27.460 --> 00:34:31.330
and making chips involves the
same skills plus a magnifying

00:34:31.330 --> 00:34:32.770
glass, right?

00:34:32.770 --> 00:34:37.179
So the private sector
can do more than we

00:34:37.179 --> 00:34:39.679
think to solve social problems.

00:34:39.679 --> 00:34:41.560
And that was it.

00:34:41.560 --> 00:34:43.510
But we opted for
the public sector.

00:34:43.510 --> 00:34:45.510
MATTHEW SPALDING: Of
course, one of the greatest

00:34:45.510 --> 00:34:47.980
spokesmen for GE was--

00:34:47.980 --> 00:34:50.800
AMITY SHLAES: Well, I tell
this story at the beginning.

00:34:50.800 --> 00:34:53.800
They had an actor
who worked for GE

00:34:53.800 --> 00:34:57.850
who was kind of old and not even
that good, a little bit corny.

00:34:57.850 --> 00:35:01.990
And he was hired to propagandize
for the free market,

00:35:01.990 --> 00:35:03.640
and that was Reagan.

00:35:03.640 --> 00:35:05.170
So Reagan, there he was.

00:35:05.170 --> 00:35:07.960
He was a middle-aged man now.

00:35:07.960 --> 00:35:13.990
And he gave not just 50 or
100, but maybe 1,000 speeches

00:35:13.990 --> 00:35:19.550
about markets to the rubber
chicken circuit of the hundred

00:35:19.550 --> 00:35:22.690
plants of General Electric
because General Electric

00:35:22.690 --> 00:35:25.090
was concerned that its
workers didn't understand

00:35:25.090 --> 00:35:28.850
that capitalism was fragile.

00:35:28.850 --> 00:35:30.370
And it all was for naught--

00:35:30.370 --> 00:35:32.830
I mean in chapter 1--
because GE suffered

00:35:32.830 --> 00:35:37.360
a terrible embarrassment in
its employees, fixed prices,

00:35:37.360 --> 00:35:38.890
and deals with Westinghouse.

00:35:38.890 --> 00:35:42.680
It was described as a
sports scandal for America

00:35:42.680 --> 00:35:46.697
because people respected GE
as they respect baseball.

00:35:46.697 --> 00:35:49.030
And it went down in the world,
and they fired the actor,

00:35:49.030 --> 00:35:51.450
and they fired his
boss, Lem Boulware.

00:35:51.450 --> 00:35:53.860
And they got rid of all
the little propaganda

00:35:53.860 --> 00:35:54.578
mill programs.

00:35:54.578 --> 00:35:55.870
And they thought, what a waste.

00:35:55.870 --> 00:35:57.810
We screwed up.

00:35:57.810 --> 00:36:01.990
But Reagan remembered and
eventually became a convert

00:36:01.990 --> 00:36:02.830
to these ideas.

00:36:02.830 --> 00:36:07.780
He came to believe in markets
by background, a New Dealer.

00:36:07.780 --> 00:36:10.090
So that story is told.

00:36:10.090 --> 00:36:12.640
MATTHEW SPALDING: So
since you brought that up,

00:36:12.640 --> 00:36:16.630
Reagan, in his 1982
diary, famously

00:36:16.630 --> 00:36:18.700
writes, "The press
is trying to paint me

00:36:18.700 --> 00:36:21.190
as trying to undo the New Deal.

00:36:21.190 --> 00:36:23.800
I remind them I voted
for FDR four times.

00:36:23.800 --> 00:36:26.500
I'm trying to undo
the Great Society.

00:36:26.500 --> 00:36:30.220
It was LBJ's war on poverty
that led to our present mess."

00:36:30.220 --> 00:36:31.810
AMITY SHLAES: Very interesting.

00:36:31.810 --> 00:36:33.685
I don't have that in
the book, I don't think.

00:36:33.685 --> 00:36:34.580
Is that-- yeah.

00:36:34.580 --> 00:36:35.080
[LAUGHTER]

00:36:35.080 --> 00:36:37.400
In the next book.

00:36:37.400 --> 00:36:38.728
So I mean, the history is--

00:36:38.728 --> 00:36:41.020
MATTHEW SPALDING: He saw the
Great Society as the enemy

00:36:41.020 --> 00:36:41.830
to be defeated.

00:36:41.830 --> 00:36:42.872
AMITY SHLAES: Absolutely.

00:36:42.872 --> 00:36:45.050
History is history, but
it's also characters.

00:36:45.050 --> 00:36:46.870
They don't have
to be presidents.

00:36:46.870 --> 00:36:48.370
They can be just the next line.

00:36:48.370 --> 00:36:50.470
So there was a book called
Best and the Brightest

00:36:50.470 --> 00:36:54.580
about the foolish errors of
arrogant men in the Vietnam

00:36:54.580 --> 00:36:57.220
War, planning the Vietnam
War and the execution,

00:36:57.220 --> 00:36:59.900
the negotiation,
the prosecution.

00:36:59.900 --> 00:37:02.080
There's a domestic Best
and the Brightest that

00:37:02.080 --> 00:37:04.660
is a bunch of guys who
were so smart they thought

00:37:04.660 --> 00:37:06.880
they could do this,
not just Sarge Shriver,

00:37:06.880 --> 00:37:08.750
but also Walter Reuther.

00:37:08.750 --> 00:37:10.400
And where is Reagan?

00:37:10.400 --> 00:37:14.200
He's like the mayors because he
became governor of California.

00:37:14.200 --> 00:37:17.560
And he had lots of things to
do, and the federal government

00:37:17.560 --> 00:37:20.030
kept getting in the way.

00:37:20.030 --> 00:37:24.760
And there's a hilarious
scene where the governors had

00:37:24.760 --> 00:37:27.940
a kind of way of vetoing a
federal project, a poverty

00:37:27.940 --> 00:37:29.950
project, but it wasn't
a really good veto.

00:37:29.950 --> 00:37:32.240
They could be
overruled, basically.

00:37:32.240 --> 00:37:34.030
So he was going to
overrule something

00:37:34.030 --> 00:37:36.170
the Nixon administration
was doing,

00:37:36.170 --> 00:37:39.130
which was funding
public interest lawyers.

00:37:39.130 --> 00:37:41.680
And Nixon didn't want
that because Nixon

00:37:41.680 --> 00:37:44.410
knew, in his heart, that Reagan
was just as popular as he was--

00:37:44.410 --> 00:37:45.670
Nixon, right?

00:37:45.670 --> 00:37:48.280
This is a very awkward
moment, and you

00:37:48.280 --> 00:37:51.490
have the lawyers for
Nixon, the president,

00:37:51.490 --> 00:37:52.900
and the lawyers for Reagan--

00:37:52.900 --> 00:37:55.030
one of them was Ed Meese--

00:37:55.030 --> 00:37:55.780
in a room.

00:37:55.780 --> 00:38:00.370
And the scary administration,
led by Frank Carlucci,

00:38:00.370 --> 00:38:04.570
said, kind of very
condescendingly to the Reagan

00:38:04.570 --> 00:38:08.050
crowd using a film
metaphor because Reagan

00:38:08.050 --> 00:38:09.780
would understand that, right?

00:38:09.780 --> 00:38:14.050
There's an A movie where
you blow this all up

00:38:14.050 --> 00:38:15.040
and we blow you up.

00:38:15.040 --> 00:38:15.820
And forgive me.

00:38:15.820 --> 00:38:17.987
I don't have the text here,
but something like that.

00:38:17.987 --> 00:38:20.110
And there's a B
movie where we make

00:38:20.110 --> 00:38:24.490
a compromise and neither of
us loses face on this "lawyers

00:38:24.490 --> 00:38:25.870
funded by Washington" thing.

00:38:25.870 --> 00:38:28.330
And maybe we'll help you with
your welfare cutbacks, which

00:38:28.330 --> 00:38:30.780
is your big pride,
Governor Reagan.

00:38:30.780 --> 00:38:33.910
And Meese goes into another room
and presumably phones Reagan,

00:38:33.910 --> 00:38:36.490
and comes back and says,
we take the B movie.

00:38:36.490 --> 00:38:38.440
[LAUGHTER]

00:38:38.440 --> 00:38:40.930
And then Reagan, I
think he sent Carlucci

00:38:40.930 --> 00:38:44.500
a bottle of an
alcoholic substance.

00:38:44.500 --> 00:38:46.390
I believe it might
have been scotch.

00:38:46.390 --> 00:38:48.730
So that was Reagan,
the governor.

00:38:48.730 --> 00:38:51.310
He decided this
had all worked out.

00:38:51.310 --> 00:38:54.698
It wasn't just scorch
and burn, right?

00:38:54.698 --> 00:38:55.990
MATTHEW SPALDING: Right, right.

00:38:55.990 --> 00:38:57.532
AMITY SHLAES: But
the welfare program

00:38:57.532 --> 00:39:00.520
became a model for welfare
reform later, Reagan's own

00:39:00.520 --> 00:39:03.330
and then also Clinton's, right?

00:39:03.330 --> 00:39:05.018
MATTHEW SPALDING:
[INAUDIBLE] yeah.

00:39:05.018 --> 00:39:05.810
AMITY SHLAES: Yeah.

00:39:08.642 --> 00:39:10.530
AUDIENCE: Ms. Shlaes,
thank you for coming.

00:39:10.530 --> 00:39:12.680
Christopher Harris with
Unhyphenated America, and I

00:39:12.680 --> 00:39:14.190
just have a quick question.

00:39:14.190 --> 00:39:16.080
My family's originally
from Detroit.

00:39:16.080 --> 00:39:21.150
And Walter Williams has talked
about the effect of the Great

00:39:21.150 --> 00:39:23.780
Society on the black
family in particular.

00:39:23.780 --> 00:39:26.563
And you talk about how the
poverty was decreasing,

00:39:26.563 --> 00:39:28.230
had decreased
dramatically part of that.

00:39:28.230 --> 00:39:29.685
And I've talked to many
of my family members

00:39:29.685 --> 00:39:31.890
and they say, look,
everybody that they grew up

00:39:31.890 --> 00:39:33.480
with-- my father's 69 years old.

00:39:33.480 --> 00:39:35.730
And basically, everybody
they grew up with

00:39:35.730 --> 00:39:37.320
had a mother and a
father and a home.

00:39:37.320 --> 00:39:38.940
I mean, there was
a handful of people

00:39:38.940 --> 00:39:41.130
who did not have a mother
and a father and a home.

00:39:41.130 --> 00:39:43.753
And afterwards-- like, I'm 46--

00:39:43.753 --> 00:39:46.170
you'll see the large percentage
of people in my generation

00:39:46.170 --> 00:39:49.180
did not grow up with a
mother and father at home.

00:39:49.180 --> 00:39:52.560
Why do you think that
the New Deal didn't

00:39:52.560 --> 00:39:56.070
have such a destructive effect
on the black family or families

00:39:56.070 --> 00:39:58.880
in general as such as
the Great Society has

00:39:58.880 --> 00:40:00.390
had on families in general?

00:40:00.390 --> 00:40:01.240
MATTHEW SPALDING:
That's a great question.

00:40:01.240 --> 00:40:02.240
AMITY SHLAES: Thank you.

00:40:02.240 --> 00:40:03.480
Great question.

00:40:03.480 --> 00:40:07.690
Actually, the family
separation issue--

00:40:11.210 --> 00:40:13.230
the most proximate
thing to which you refer

00:40:13.230 --> 00:40:18.100
is to get welfare money, you
had to be a single parent.

00:40:18.100 --> 00:40:19.540
And the welfare money--

00:40:19.540 --> 00:40:23.310
and then, on top of that,
to get subsidized housing.

00:40:23.310 --> 00:40:27.330
So some of that was
pre-Great Society.

00:40:27.330 --> 00:40:30.150
It was state welfare
law, for example,

00:40:30.150 --> 00:40:32.490
which said we won't
support men who earn,

00:40:32.490 --> 00:40:35.940
but we'll support families
with dependent children

00:40:35.940 --> 00:40:37.630
and no worker mom.

00:40:37.630 --> 00:40:42.450
So it was a perverse outcome
from a well-meaning plan.

00:40:42.450 --> 00:40:46.260
And in my description of
Pruitt-Igoe, this housing

00:40:46.260 --> 00:40:48.240
project, which was
rather like the housing

00:40:48.240 --> 00:40:51.840
projects in Detroit, the dads
had to hide in the closet

00:40:51.840 --> 00:40:54.930
when the social worker came
because they would lose

00:40:54.930 --> 00:40:59.910
not only the money, but also
the family would the apartment

00:40:59.910 --> 00:41:01.590
if the dad were discovered.

00:41:01.590 --> 00:41:04.500
And where is the
dad supposed to go?

00:41:04.500 --> 00:41:06.930
Far away and get a job?

00:41:06.930 --> 00:41:10.410
That was a huge factor
which the Great Society,

00:41:10.410 --> 00:41:12.210
through the format
of its funding,

00:41:12.210 --> 00:41:14.160
perpetuated and strengthened.

00:41:14.160 --> 00:41:15.750
And it wasn't just
black families.

00:41:15.750 --> 00:41:17.070
It was white families as well.

00:41:17.070 --> 00:41:18.528
I would add another
factor, though.

00:41:18.528 --> 00:41:22.140
I really don't
like urban renewal.

00:41:22.140 --> 00:41:23.400
That was my note.

00:41:23.400 --> 00:41:25.410
I'm paraphrasing, but
it says, urban renewal

00:41:25.410 --> 00:41:28.230
is minority removal.

00:41:28.230 --> 00:41:31.200
And when the riots
happened in Detroit,

00:41:31.200 --> 00:41:35.280
it was said it was the people
who had been bulldozed out

00:41:35.280 --> 00:41:39.270
of old neighborhoods
which were not only poor,

00:41:39.270 --> 00:41:43.800
not only occasionally slums,
but also lively and much loved,

00:41:43.800 --> 00:41:45.630
bulldozed out of
their homes to be put

00:41:45.630 --> 00:41:48.390
in sterile vertical projects.

00:41:48.390 --> 00:41:50.760
And they were angry and sad.

00:41:50.760 --> 00:41:52.590
They were displaced
and displaced.

00:41:52.590 --> 00:41:53.940
They were displaced by slavery.

00:41:53.940 --> 00:41:55.898
Then they were displaced
when they moved north.

00:41:55.898 --> 00:41:58.068
And then they were displaced
again from that place

00:41:58.068 --> 00:42:00.360
where they originally lived
when they moved to Detroit.

00:42:00.360 --> 00:42:03.430
That's a lot of
displacement for a family.

00:42:03.430 --> 00:42:10.000
So I try to describe
that in the book,

00:42:10.000 --> 00:42:14.020
and I very much appreciate
your raising that issue.

00:42:14.020 --> 00:42:16.020
MATTHEW SPALDING: Another
character in your book

00:42:16.020 --> 00:42:18.210
is Daniel Patrick Moynihan
who, of course, wrote

00:42:18.210 --> 00:42:20.610
a report on the black family
and then was later vilified.

00:42:20.610 --> 00:42:21.840
Tell us about him.

00:42:21.840 --> 00:42:23.940
AMITY SHLAES: The first
person in my lifetime

00:42:23.940 --> 00:42:29.190
I know about who was ever
canceled, Daniel Patrick

00:42:29.190 --> 00:42:33.870
Moynihan because he wrote a
book trying to describe trouble

00:42:33.870 --> 00:42:36.930
in a single-parent family,
particularly maybe when

00:42:36.930 --> 00:42:39.780
the father, who departed
or is infrequently present,

00:42:39.780 --> 00:42:41.270
is alcoholic.

00:42:41.270 --> 00:42:44.640
And by the way, that's Daniel
Patrick Moynihan's life.

00:42:44.640 --> 00:42:46.560
He just happens to be white.

00:42:46.560 --> 00:42:50.130
So his father was not there,
and there were other fathers

00:42:50.130 --> 00:42:50.850
occasionally.

00:42:50.850 --> 00:42:54.870
And this was problematic
for the Moynihans.

00:42:54.870 --> 00:42:57.390
So he tried to describe
that question which you just

00:42:57.390 --> 00:42:58.350
put your finger on.

00:42:58.350 --> 00:43:03.690
And pretty soon, it became to
seem condescending for a white

00:43:03.690 --> 00:43:06.240
person to describe
something that, what,

00:43:06.240 --> 00:43:08.700
is supposed to be uniquely
black, which it isn't.

00:43:08.700 --> 00:43:11.280
And he was shut out
by the Democrats.

00:43:11.280 --> 00:43:14.850
And so I spend a lot
of time on Moynihan

00:43:14.850 --> 00:43:17.497
and his humiliation
and loneliness

00:43:17.497 --> 00:43:19.830
because he was actually a
very insightful guy with a lot

00:43:19.830 --> 00:43:21.030
to contribute to government.

00:43:21.030 --> 00:43:24.810
And his revenge was to come
back working for Richard Nixon

00:43:24.810 --> 00:43:27.600
and offer a program that sounds
very familiar today, which

00:43:27.600 --> 00:43:29.820
is guaranteed income
for whites and blacks

00:43:29.820 --> 00:43:32.190
below a certain income level.

00:43:32.190 --> 00:43:38.140
But the canceling of Moynihan
by the world and Cambridge,

00:43:38.140 --> 00:43:41.838
Massachusetts, is a bitter
and fiery thing to behold.

00:43:41.838 --> 00:43:43.630
MATTHEW SPALDING: This
gentleman over here.

00:43:43.630 --> 00:43:44.130
Yes, sir.

00:43:48.280 --> 00:43:50.980
AUDIENCE: Thank you very
much for your talk tonight.

00:43:50.980 --> 00:43:52.090
One question.

00:43:52.090 --> 00:43:53.590
So at the very
beginning, you talked

00:43:53.590 --> 00:43:55.720
about how the early
Progressives were very much more

00:43:55.720 --> 00:43:56.650
focused on the states.

00:43:56.650 --> 00:44:00.220
It was very much more of a
smaller level than federal.

00:44:00.220 --> 00:44:02.327
And it sounds to me
like later on, it's

00:44:02.327 --> 00:44:03.910
really with the Great
Society that you

00:44:03.910 --> 00:44:10.180
see the progressive thought take
root in that federal portion.

00:44:10.180 --> 00:44:12.680
Woodrow Wilson talks
very much in his writings

00:44:12.680 --> 00:44:13.930
about the idea of a hive mind.

00:44:13.930 --> 00:44:17.800
He talks about how there's
this general aggregate where

00:44:17.800 --> 00:44:20.440
everything centralizes
in Washington.

00:44:20.440 --> 00:44:23.710
And to provide an
analogy, a lot of people

00:44:23.710 --> 00:44:26.890
provide how Lincoln almost
acts as a fulfillment

00:44:26.890 --> 00:44:28.580
for the American founding.

00:44:28.580 --> 00:44:30.790
So in a similar way,
if you want to think

00:44:30.790 --> 00:44:33.820
of the progressive
thought with Wilson

00:44:33.820 --> 00:44:36.730
as a re-founding or
revolution, could you

00:44:36.730 --> 00:44:39.010
say almost that that
fulfillment comes about

00:44:39.010 --> 00:44:41.260
in the Great Society?

00:44:41.260 --> 00:44:42.570
AMITY SHLAES: Yes.

00:44:42.570 --> 00:44:43.470
[LAUGHTER]

00:44:43.470 --> 00:44:44.533
Another question.

00:44:44.533 --> 00:44:46.450
MATTHEW SPALDING: Would
you care to elaborate?

00:44:46.450 --> 00:44:48.510
AMITY SHLAES:
[INAUDIBLE] remember--

00:44:48.510 --> 00:44:51.787
did you ever read God and Man
at Yale by William F. Buckley?

00:44:51.787 --> 00:44:54.120
I never read it because I
wasn't interested particularly

00:44:54.120 --> 00:44:55.080
in the God part.

00:44:55.080 --> 00:44:56.340
But what it's really--

00:44:56.340 --> 00:45:00.090
the meat of it was they
changed the way they

00:45:00.090 --> 00:45:02.220
teach economics in college.

00:45:02.220 --> 00:45:03.090
Gee whiz.

00:45:03.090 --> 00:45:06.120
We used to teach about
individuals and--

00:45:06.120 --> 00:45:08.130
I don't know-- the
invisible hand and so on.

00:45:08.130 --> 00:45:10.440
And now, all of a sudden,
it's all about planning.

00:45:10.440 --> 00:45:16.230
So that commenced this big
sea change at universities

00:45:16.230 --> 00:45:18.420
right after World
War II, which meant

00:45:18.420 --> 00:45:22.590
15 years worth of
undergraduates had

00:45:22.590 --> 00:45:26.250
been schooled in this
planning way of thinking.

00:45:26.250 --> 00:45:31.670
So now the grownups didn't
know much about free markets

00:45:31.670 --> 00:45:33.340
or care about them.

00:45:33.340 --> 00:45:35.480
They knew about planning.

00:45:35.480 --> 00:45:40.850
And so those people were
ready to takeover around 1960.

00:45:40.850 --> 00:45:44.650
They had become the
grownups and the leaders.

00:45:44.650 --> 00:45:47.450
MATTHEW SPALDING:
Yeah, and that planning

00:45:47.450 --> 00:45:50.570
grows out of the philosophical
roots of the early progressives

00:45:50.570 --> 00:45:53.450
who thought that
progressive experts could

00:45:53.450 --> 00:45:55.310
solve all problems.

00:45:55.310 --> 00:45:56.720
AMITY SHLAES: Right.

00:45:56.720 --> 00:45:59.510
And also, there's a
cumulative effect.

00:45:59.510 --> 00:46:02.120
Planning upon planning
yields perversity.

00:46:02.120 --> 00:46:03.800
Oh, we had that
reform last year.

00:46:03.800 --> 00:46:07.220
Oh, this is the 17th
definition of child

00:46:07.220 --> 00:46:10.400
we've had in the tax code, and
each one contradicts the other,

00:46:10.400 --> 00:46:12.615
and five are still [INAUDIBLE].

00:46:12.615 --> 00:46:14.990
MATTHEW SPALDING: It also goes
back to the fellow's point

00:46:14.990 --> 00:46:17.120
here, which is
they always, oh, it

00:46:17.120 --> 00:46:19.670
didn't work because there was
some factor that was not right.

00:46:19.670 --> 00:46:20.510
We'll try it again.

00:46:20.510 --> 00:46:20.960
AMITY SHLAES: We'll try--

00:46:20.960 --> 00:46:21.460
right.

00:46:21.460 --> 00:46:24.798
This is like a book with a bunch
of progressive waves within it,

00:46:24.798 --> 00:46:26.090
and they get bigger and bigger.

00:46:26.090 --> 00:46:28.488
And the magnitudes of
dollars get bigger.

00:46:28.488 --> 00:46:30.530
Because at the very
beginning, Michael Harrington

00:46:30.530 --> 00:46:31.940
hears from Sargent Shriver.

00:46:31.940 --> 00:46:36.470
They're going to get a
billion dollars for welfare.

00:46:36.470 --> 00:46:40.853
And Michael Harrington says,
that's just nickel and dimed.

00:46:40.853 --> 00:46:42.020
We need more than a billion.

00:46:42.020 --> 00:46:44.062
And Sargent Shriver says,
I don't know about you,

00:46:44.062 --> 00:46:47.150
young man, but I never had
a billion to spend before.

00:46:47.150 --> 00:46:48.590
I think it feels pretty big.

00:46:48.590 --> 00:46:52.040
But of course, the numbers
did go Harrington-wise

00:46:52.040 --> 00:46:53.520
into the mag--

00:46:53.520 --> 00:46:55.815
even add a few zeros.

00:46:55.815 --> 00:46:57.440
MATTHEW SPALDING: So
that conversation,

00:46:57.440 --> 00:46:58.905
just not to be a
spoiler, but it's

00:46:58.905 --> 00:47:00.530
the very beginning
of the book, and she

00:47:00.530 --> 00:47:02.690
doesn't identify who
the two people are

00:47:02.690 --> 00:47:05.300
until you get several pages
into the conversation.

00:47:05.300 --> 00:47:07.190
And it sounds eerily
like a conversation

00:47:07.190 --> 00:47:10.610
you might hear today in
the halls of Congress

00:47:10.610 --> 00:47:16.580
about very large programs,
very large idealistic ideas

00:47:16.580 --> 00:47:20.750
about what government
ought to be doing.

00:47:20.750 --> 00:47:23.290
Why didn't we learn
any of these lessons?

00:47:23.290 --> 00:47:24.600
Why are we doing this again?

00:47:24.600 --> 00:47:25.530
AMITY SHLAES: Oh.

00:47:25.530 --> 00:47:27.140
MATTHEW SPALDING: [INAUDIBLE]

00:47:27.140 --> 00:47:29.040
AMITY SHLAES: Well,
the motto of the book,

00:47:29.040 --> 00:47:33.260
which I will say I have
heard Chanel, Coco Chanel,

00:47:33.260 --> 00:47:38.960
say, but also other important
people, nothing is new.

00:47:38.960 --> 00:47:42.050
It is just forgotten.

00:47:42.050 --> 00:47:43.110
Nothing is new.

00:47:43.110 --> 00:47:44.083
It is just forgotten.

00:47:44.083 --> 00:47:46.250
The grammar's a little
awkward, but I still like it.

00:47:46.250 --> 00:47:49.550
Nothing is new, but
it is just forgotten.

00:47:49.550 --> 00:47:52.858
We forgot the '70s, which
were the consequence.

00:47:52.858 --> 00:47:55.150
MATTHEW SPALDING: Well, they
were a forgettable decade.

00:47:55.150 --> 00:47:56.500
AMITY SHLAES: They were
definitely forgettable.

00:47:56.500 --> 00:47:57.410
[LAUGHTER]

00:47:57.410 --> 00:48:01.520
So I try to explain the '70s
to myself and other people.

00:48:01.520 --> 00:48:04.820
The '70s was a period when we
thought our houses would be

00:48:04.820 --> 00:48:07.540
smaller than our parents--

00:48:07.540 --> 00:48:10.220
and they technically
were at certain points.

00:48:10.220 --> 00:48:12.830
And we had two fewer
bedrooms than we actually

00:48:12.830 --> 00:48:17.050
needed because the
interest rate was 18%.

00:48:17.050 --> 00:48:20.410
That it was a period
where we didn't think

00:48:20.410 --> 00:48:23.020
we would ever have enough
energy to heat our homes.

00:48:23.020 --> 00:48:25.865
And I don't even want
to write the '70s

00:48:25.865 --> 00:48:26.990
because it's a little dark.

00:48:26.990 --> 00:48:28.270
I have to figure
out a happy '70s.

00:48:28.270 --> 00:48:28.990
MATTHEW SPALDING: [LAUGHS]

00:48:28.990 --> 00:48:31.448
AMITY SHLAES: But you think
of, those of you will remember,

00:48:31.448 --> 00:48:33.280
the ratcheting down
of expectation,

00:48:33.280 --> 00:48:36.610
the emphasis on
scarcity, is reminiscent

00:48:36.610 --> 00:48:39.190
of the current
environmental movement.

00:48:39.190 --> 00:48:43.060
I mean, there was a philosopher
named Paul Ehrlich who actually

00:48:43.060 --> 00:48:47.260
believed there should be a
very heavy tax on diapers

00:48:47.260 --> 00:48:49.240
so that people would
have fewer babies.

00:48:49.240 --> 00:48:52.750
And also, we should change the
tax code-- no child credit,

00:48:52.750 --> 00:48:53.740
none of that--

00:48:53.740 --> 00:48:58.150
and to discourage reproduction
because we wouldn't

00:48:58.150 --> 00:48:59.260
be able to feed ourselves.

00:48:59.260 --> 00:49:01.540
And more importantly, we
were the world's model.

00:49:01.540 --> 00:49:05.080
And how about introducing
the vasectomy?

00:49:05.080 --> 00:49:10.300
And how about giving a lot of
incentives for that procedure?

00:49:10.300 --> 00:49:12.920
And I find it very
creepy, looking back,

00:49:12.920 --> 00:49:14.230
particularly their hubris.

00:49:14.230 --> 00:49:15.970
And of course,
other countries took

00:49:15.970 --> 00:49:20.140
what we recommended via our
World Bank and Mr. McNamara

00:49:20.140 --> 00:49:22.460
and did awful things
with their populations.

00:49:22.460 --> 00:49:25.420
You're thinking of India
and the sterilizations that

00:49:25.420 --> 00:49:27.940
were virtually forced
upon people there

00:49:27.940 --> 00:49:33.143
as part of our progressive
ideal of population reduction.

00:49:33.143 --> 00:49:35.310
MATTHEW SPALDING: Let's get
a couple more questions.

00:49:35.310 --> 00:49:39.250
Fellow back there, and then
there's on up here too.

00:49:39.250 --> 00:49:40.540
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:49:40.540 --> 00:49:45.130
So my question is, it seems
that with the Great Society,

00:49:45.130 --> 00:49:51.160
there is a certain
appeal to nobility.

00:49:51.160 --> 00:49:54.730
There's a higher aim towards
it, the eradication of poverty.

00:49:54.730 --> 00:49:57.790
So that's a very
idealistic sentiment,

00:49:57.790 --> 00:49:59.500
but also a noble one.

00:49:59.500 --> 00:50:03.730
Is there a way of introducing
a nobility into free market

00:50:03.730 --> 00:50:07.750
economics that simply isn't
rooted in self-interest

00:50:07.750 --> 00:50:13.720
to provide a sort of greater
appeal that would move folks

00:50:13.720 --> 00:50:15.195
in my generation--

00:50:15.195 --> 00:50:17.320
I'm a little younger than
some of the people here--

00:50:17.320 --> 00:50:23.363
and allow them to move
forward in a productive way?

00:50:23.363 --> 00:50:25.780
AMITY SHLAES: Well, I think
one thing you're talking about

00:50:25.780 --> 00:50:30.190
is the transactional
nature of Silicon Valley.

00:50:30.190 --> 00:50:32.200
I'll never know the
counterparty in this deal.

00:50:32.200 --> 00:50:36.860
I'm selling him x, y, z,
and I don't care about him.

00:50:36.860 --> 00:50:39.070
But that's not most of commerce.

00:50:39.070 --> 00:50:45.220
Most of free market is everyone
you meet, you will meet again.

00:50:45.220 --> 00:50:47.800
Every person you ever
meet in your life

00:50:47.800 --> 00:50:49.660
will remember the
encounter with you

00:50:49.660 --> 00:50:52.240
and how you handled
the transaction.

00:50:52.240 --> 00:50:54.520
And were you kind?

00:50:54.520 --> 00:50:58.160
And did you under-deliver
or over-deliver?

00:50:58.160 --> 00:50:59.892
That's what one
always tells students.

00:50:59.892 --> 00:51:01.600
You think you'll never
see someone again.

00:51:01.600 --> 00:51:04.390
You will always
see everyone again,

00:51:04.390 --> 00:51:06.190
and everyone will
always remember

00:51:06.190 --> 00:51:09.010
your tie, what color
it was, or your scarf,

00:51:09.010 --> 00:51:11.290
and were you polite or did
you talk to someone else,

00:51:11.290 --> 00:51:12.620
and did you cheat him.

00:51:12.620 --> 00:51:15.950
So there is a natural
morality to commerce.

00:51:15.950 --> 00:51:19.150
Because when you sell
something to someone,

00:51:19.150 --> 00:51:24.340
they remember that,
if you cheated them.

00:51:24.340 --> 00:51:28.180
Life, most of us
avoid bad airports

00:51:28.180 --> 00:51:30.370
because bad airports lie to us.

00:51:30.370 --> 00:51:34.000
They advertise a close
gate, and then we

00:51:34.000 --> 00:51:36.620
find we have to walk
2 miles to the gate.

00:51:36.620 --> 00:51:38.950
They say we have
a boarding pass,

00:51:38.950 --> 00:51:42.160
and then we're unseated, right?

00:51:42.160 --> 00:51:45.770
So we know, when we can
to avoid bad airports,

00:51:45.770 --> 00:51:49.930
which are, say,
low-trust places.

00:51:49.930 --> 00:51:51.610
And we look for
high-trust places

00:51:51.610 --> 00:51:54.940
where the airport is even
nicer than we thought,

00:51:54.940 --> 00:51:57.460
and maybe it has free coffee.

00:51:57.460 --> 00:51:59.890
I think they have
that in Toronto.

00:51:59.890 --> 00:52:03.100
So I like that, and
that's not socialism.

00:52:03.100 --> 00:52:05.410
That's good commerce, right?

00:52:05.410 --> 00:52:12.590
So I think we have to
think about the kindness

00:52:12.590 --> 00:52:18.830
and the morality in
commerce, and expose it.

00:52:18.830 --> 00:52:21.470
Let's think of it
this way. eBay--

00:52:21.470 --> 00:52:24.260
when we were young,
people my age,

00:52:24.260 --> 00:52:27.140
again, we wouldn't have
believed that eBay could work.

00:52:27.140 --> 00:52:29.450
Because you send your
money off to someone

00:52:29.450 --> 00:52:32.060
in another state
who nobody you know

00:52:32.060 --> 00:52:35.540
knows, and he or she sends you
back more or less the thing

00:52:35.540 --> 00:52:37.350
you ordered, right?

00:52:37.350 --> 00:52:38.330
More or less.

00:52:38.330 --> 00:52:41.870
And sometimes it's a part which
you haven't inspected yourself,

00:52:41.870 --> 00:52:43.800
like a car part.

00:52:43.800 --> 00:52:46.192
Oh, my gosh, and
it might be flawed

00:52:46.192 --> 00:52:47.900
in some way where that
other person could

00:52:47.900 --> 00:52:49.800
say he technically
sent you a good thing

00:52:49.800 --> 00:52:51.380
but the thing is useless to you.

00:52:51.380 --> 00:52:57.800
And we might send $185 for a car
part if we're building a car.

00:52:57.800 --> 00:53:01.550
And yet, most of the
time, eBay works.

00:53:01.550 --> 00:53:03.890
Most of the time, most
of us are satisfied

00:53:03.890 --> 00:53:07.790
with eBay and say, that is an
incredibly wonderful economy

00:53:07.790 --> 00:53:09.470
where the people don't
cheat each other.

00:53:09.470 --> 00:53:11.540
There's a kind of broker ref.

00:53:11.540 --> 00:53:14.300
That would be the eBay company.

00:53:14.300 --> 00:53:16.160
And most of the time--

00:53:16.160 --> 00:53:18.950
and the same is for Airbnb.

00:53:18.950 --> 00:53:21.500
Most of the time, you
don't get murdered.

00:53:21.500 --> 00:53:23.690
[LAUGHTER]

00:53:23.690 --> 00:53:26.600
Most of the time, even
on Craigslist, you

00:53:26.600 --> 00:53:29.630
don't get-- now, most of
the time on Craigslist,

00:53:29.630 --> 00:53:31.600
you don't get cheated.

00:53:31.600 --> 00:53:35.270
The stories that you hear in
Uber, they're the exception.

00:53:35.270 --> 00:53:39.410
So there's a lot of evidence
of a great commercial economy

00:53:39.410 --> 00:53:41.780
involving a lot of
kindness and respect

00:53:41.780 --> 00:53:45.130
for fellow men and a lot
of utils to put it in,

00:53:45.130 --> 00:53:50.610
a lot of benefit to people,
that is underappreciated.

00:53:54.530 --> 00:53:58.370
MATTHEW SPALDING: I was thinking
of Reagan's great retort

00:53:58.370 --> 00:54:01.347
was the Creative Society.

00:54:01.347 --> 00:54:02.180
AMITY SHLAES: Right.

00:54:02.180 --> 00:54:04.330
Reagan is a very sad
character in this book.

00:54:04.330 --> 00:54:08.070
He loses his job at GE, and
he can't get any good roles.

00:54:08.070 --> 00:54:10.910
But he does give a good
speech for Barry Goldwater,

00:54:10.910 --> 00:54:13.760
and then he decides he might
run for governor of California.

00:54:13.760 --> 00:54:15.660
And he says, what am
I going to call it?

00:54:15.660 --> 00:54:18.590
And he actually
goes to a minister,

00:54:18.590 --> 00:54:20.870
and they come up with--
or the minister already

00:54:20.870 --> 00:54:25.090
has and offers
Creative Society, which

00:54:25.090 --> 00:54:26.680
is what the companies are.

00:54:26.680 --> 00:54:29.230
Do think the Intel people--
when they started and were

00:54:29.230 --> 00:54:31.690
at Fairchild, did they
think they could ever

00:54:31.690 --> 00:54:34.000
invent something that
would be so useful?

00:54:34.000 --> 00:54:34.570
No.

00:54:34.570 --> 00:54:37.390
They also believed
that everything

00:54:37.390 --> 00:54:42.007
you ever did in their area
was in service of government.

00:54:42.007 --> 00:54:43.840
They were stuck in the
military industrial--

00:54:43.840 --> 00:54:46.750
what do you build computer
parts, electronics for?

00:54:46.750 --> 00:54:47.740
The government.

00:54:47.740 --> 00:54:50.410
Only the government can
have a big computer, right?

00:54:50.410 --> 00:54:51.250
MATTHEW SPALDING: No one
else would use these.

00:54:51.250 --> 00:54:52.875
AMITY SHLAES: Nobody
else would use it.

00:54:52.875 --> 00:54:54.670
And what they realized--
and I follow them,

00:54:54.670 --> 00:54:56.200
and you can see it
dawning on them.

00:54:56.200 --> 00:55:00.050
And it is like the sun
coming into their life--

00:55:00.050 --> 00:55:04.030
is somebody might buy what I'm
making who's not a government.

00:55:04.030 --> 00:55:06.130
And somebody might
buy enough of it

00:55:06.130 --> 00:55:07.870
that I would have
money for research

00:55:07.870 --> 00:55:10.930
to invent something that
wouldn't be for government.

00:55:10.930 --> 00:55:14.050
That's the story of Bob
Noyce and Gordon Moore.

00:55:14.050 --> 00:55:15.310
They have a few epiphanies.

00:55:15.310 --> 00:55:18.640
One is Moore's Law, which is you
can make smaller chips pretty

00:55:18.640 --> 00:55:20.060
soon.

00:55:20.060 --> 00:55:23.470
And there's a kind of
geometric acceleration there.

00:55:23.470 --> 00:55:26.620
But the other is, wow,
big companies that

00:55:26.620 --> 00:55:30.700
make technical things in STEM
actually serve regular people,

00:55:30.700 --> 00:55:33.370
and there's a tremendous
relief at that discovery.

00:55:33.370 --> 00:55:35.620
And Reagan doesn't
really get it.

00:55:35.620 --> 00:55:37.090
He only sort of gets it.

00:55:37.090 --> 00:55:40.360
But he has an
intuitive appreciation

00:55:40.360 --> 00:55:43.430
for the entrepreneurial
because to be an actor,

00:55:43.430 --> 00:55:45.520
you have to be entrepreneurial.

00:55:45.520 --> 00:55:47.895
You have a lot of humiliation,
a lot of highs, a lot of--

00:55:47.895 --> 00:55:49.353
MATTHEW SPALDING:
A lot of failure.

00:55:49.353 --> 00:55:50.930
AMITY SHLAES: A lot of failure.

00:55:50.930 --> 00:55:52.610
MATTHEW SPALDING: Yes, sir.

00:55:52.610 --> 00:55:54.610
AUDIENCE: To what
extent do you credit--

00:55:54.610 --> 00:55:55.050
AMITY SHLAES: Where--

00:55:55.050 --> 00:55:55.920
AUDIENCE: Sorry, right--

00:55:55.920 --> 00:55:56.340
AMITY SHLAES: Oh, [INAUDIBLE].

00:55:56.340 --> 00:55:56.780
MATTHEW SPALDING: Right here.

00:55:56.780 --> 00:55:57.947
AUDIENCE: Right here, sorry.

00:55:57.947 --> 00:55:59.930
To what extent do you credit--

00:55:59.930 --> 00:56:02.230
because going on parallel
to the start of the Great

00:56:02.230 --> 00:56:05.860
Society is the civil
rights movement.

00:56:05.860 --> 00:56:08.110
To what extent do
you credit the fact

00:56:08.110 --> 00:56:11.290
that the civil rights
movement now would largely

00:56:11.290 --> 00:56:15.920
be seen as a positive
use of federal power

00:56:15.920 --> 00:56:18.430
over localities in
order to achieve

00:56:18.430 --> 00:56:25.430
a common good of expanding civil
rights throughout the nation

00:56:25.430 --> 00:56:32.000
as a convincing mechanism for
people who might otherwise have

00:56:32.000 --> 00:56:35.960
been cautious as a society of
what the federal government can

00:56:35.960 --> 00:56:39.620
do and might have had a greater
trust in their localities?

00:56:39.620 --> 00:56:45.920
The civil rights movement
seems to have, to me, reversed

00:56:45.920 --> 00:56:48.560
the presumption that
the localities were,

00:56:48.560 --> 00:56:49.580
in fact, trustworthy.

00:56:49.580 --> 00:56:52.970
AMITY SHLAES: Right, because
only 8% of people, black,

00:56:52.970 --> 00:56:56.960
are registered to vote in
Mississippi, a tragedy,

00:56:56.960 --> 00:57:01.220
at the beginning, it's
wrong, needs fixed, right?

00:57:01.220 --> 00:57:05.480
Therefore, we must bus
every kid in every city

00:57:05.480 --> 00:57:07.340
across the northern
part of America,

00:57:07.340 --> 00:57:08.690
is what you're describing.

00:57:08.690 --> 00:57:11.210
Because the federal
government intervened--

00:57:11.210 --> 00:57:13.355
and some of those feel rightly--

00:57:13.355 --> 00:57:16.760
with the Civil Rights Act and
then the Voting Rights Act,

00:57:16.760 --> 00:57:21.320
which was subsequent,
we gave it license

00:57:21.320 --> 00:57:24.980
to intervene everywhere and
write policy for all towns.

00:57:24.980 --> 00:57:26.260
Yeah, of course, I credit it.

00:57:26.260 --> 00:57:28.460
It's a hard call
because something

00:57:28.460 --> 00:57:33.650
had to happen in the south
if only 8% of a certain group

00:57:33.650 --> 00:57:35.130
are registered to vote.

00:57:35.130 --> 00:57:36.500
I don't know how to fix that.

00:57:36.500 --> 00:57:39.530
In the book, what I say is the
Voting Rights Act and the Civil

00:57:39.530 --> 00:57:43.050
Rights Law, I don't
think were negative.

00:57:43.050 --> 00:57:43.850
They were positive.

00:57:43.850 --> 00:57:48.680
But when we began to move, as
Johnson did with his 1960--

00:57:48.680 --> 00:57:52.010
I think it's '65 speech at
Howard University, where

00:57:52.010 --> 00:57:54.500
he basically signed up
for affirmative action

00:57:54.500 --> 00:57:56.690
and something beyond
equality of opportunity,

00:57:56.690 --> 00:57:58.580
moving to equality
of result, that

00:57:58.580 --> 00:58:01.220
was an overstep because you
always disappoint people when

00:58:01.220 --> 00:58:04.955
you promise equality of result.

00:58:04.955 --> 00:58:07.200
MATTHEW SPALDING: Amity,
this is wonderful.

00:58:07.200 --> 00:58:08.430
I'm thoroughly enjoying it--

00:58:08.430 --> 00:58:10.300
the people, the stories.

00:58:10.300 --> 00:58:13.410
I'm going to give the
last point to you.

00:58:13.410 --> 00:58:15.960
What would you like
to add or suggest

00:58:15.960 --> 00:58:17.250
that we've not talked about?

00:58:21.240 --> 00:58:24.000
AMITY SHLAES: I think the
thing that surprised me most

00:58:24.000 --> 00:58:27.270
in writing this book was the
importance of labor unions.

00:58:27.270 --> 00:58:29.560
Labor unions are
hard to understand.

00:58:29.560 --> 00:58:31.590
They have their own language.

00:58:31.590 --> 00:58:34.310
They have their own
court, the NLRB.

00:58:34.310 --> 00:58:36.540
What is-- it most of
the time, most people

00:58:36.540 --> 00:58:38.380
don't understand
what's going on.

00:58:38.380 --> 00:58:40.620
But what happened,
basically in the '60s,

00:58:40.620 --> 00:58:43.710
was the labor unions were the
core of the Democratic Party,

00:58:43.710 --> 00:58:46.260
much more so than today,
as many of you know,

00:58:46.260 --> 00:58:48.870
because of the way our laws
have changed and some restraint

00:58:48.870 --> 00:58:51.810
and because of the weakness
of our labor unions.

00:58:51.810 --> 00:58:56.440
And they decided what happened.

00:58:56.440 --> 00:58:59.370
An example, there was
a big student movement

00:58:59.370 --> 00:59:03.460
that started at Port
Huron, Michigan.

00:59:03.460 --> 00:59:06.043
The Port Huron
Statement is legendary.

00:59:06.043 --> 00:59:07.710
And what I didn't
know till I researched

00:59:07.710 --> 00:59:10.440
this book was that
Port Huron meeting

00:59:10.440 --> 00:59:13.920
of Tom Hayden, who
became a legendary SDS

00:59:13.920 --> 00:59:18.090
person, and Michael
Harrington and a few others,

00:59:18.090 --> 00:59:24.150
was essentially made possible
by the AFL-CIO and the UAW.

00:59:24.150 --> 00:59:29.340
Not only that, but Hayden
was later paid by the UAW

00:59:29.340 --> 00:59:33.530
to do the little mischief he
did in Newark, New Jersey.

00:59:33.530 --> 00:59:36.465
And what is tragic
and a good story

00:59:36.465 --> 00:59:39.360
is Walter Reuther, the head
of the United Auto Workers,

00:59:39.360 --> 00:59:44.370
I regard as a kind
of fool, flawed hero.

00:59:44.370 --> 00:59:48.210
Walter Reuther just thought,
well, let's have a youth wing.

00:59:48.210 --> 00:59:49.543
What's wrong with funding youth?

00:59:49.543 --> 00:59:51.252
A lot of them are
going to go to college.

00:59:51.252 --> 00:59:53.100
Well, that's more
members for my union.

00:59:53.100 --> 00:59:55.590
Maybe I could have 2 million
members instead of 1 million

00:59:55.590 --> 00:59:58.470
in the United Auto Workers and
squeeze out those Teamsters.

00:59:58.470 --> 01:00:01.098
So I'm going to put them on
a long leash, those students.

01:00:01.098 --> 01:00:01.890
Well, they're wild.

01:00:01.890 --> 01:00:02.880
Well, I was wild.

01:00:02.880 --> 01:00:04.350
I went to Russia.

01:00:04.350 --> 01:00:06.210
They're wild, and I'll
look the other way

01:00:06.210 --> 01:00:08.760
and hope that if I fund
them and love them and be

01:00:08.760 --> 01:00:11.280
like their dad,
then later they'll

01:00:11.280 --> 01:00:12.910
come and be loyal to me.

01:00:12.910 --> 01:00:15.060
And instead, what
those students did--

01:00:15.060 --> 01:00:17.920
the Students for a
Democratic Society was wild.

01:00:17.920 --> 01:00:20.700
They did ruin the
Chicago convention-- was

01:00:20.700 --> 01:00:25.960
hand the election of 1968 to
Walter Reuther's opponent.

01:00:25.960 --> 01:00:27.180
Oh, so bitter.

01:00:27.180 --> 01:00:29.430
Imagine the Passover scenes.

01:00:29.430 --> 01:00:32.520
Imagine the family dinners.

01:00:32.520 --> 01:00:35.250
Reuther just couldn't
believe that the youth

01:00:35.250 --> 01:00:37.410
would turn against
him, he who did

01:00:37.410 --> 01:00:39.750
so much for them and his union.

01:00:39.750 --> 01:00:41.700
And what's very
sad about Reuther

01:00:41.700 --> 01:00:46.950
was he actually died
not of disappointment,

01:00:46.950 --> 01:00:51.990
but in one of those rickety,
wrong, early Learjets.

01:00:51.990 --> 01:00:54.300
He was in a terrible
plane crash going

01:00:54.300 --> 01:00:57.840
to a kind of utopian
place in Black

01:00:57.840 --> 01:00:59.640
Lake, Michigan
that he had built,

01:00:59.640 --> 01:01:01.680
a retreat for union workers.

01:01:01.680 --> 01:01:05.730
But it is symbolic because his
dream of a Social Democratic

01:01:05.730 --> 01:01:09.750
America also died at that point.

01:01:09.750 --> 01:01:11.490
So that, I didn't know.

01:01:11.490 --> 01:01:15.900
And if you come from the Midwest
and are of a certain age,

01:01:15.900 --> 01:01:19.680
you heard Walter Reuther's name
every night of your childhood,

01:01:19.680 --> 01:01:21.750
along with the Vietnam
War count, right?

01:01:21.750 --> 01:01:23.910
The casualties were
and Walter Reuther

01:01:23.910 --> 01:01:26.880
said, because the unions
were so important.

01:01:26.880 --> 01:01:29.400
So I will say, I learned
a lot about them.

01:01:29.400 --> 01:01:30.680
They're not that complicated.

01:01:30.680 --> 01:01:32.860
They were wrong.

01:01:32.860 --> 01:01:34.890
They're an example
of lovable people

01:01:34.890 --> 01:01:38.370
trying to help other people and
doing something that does not

01:01:38.370 --> 01:01:40.325
have a positive result.

01:01:40.325 --> 01:01:42.700
MATTHEW SPALDING: Amity, it's
been great having you here.

01:01:42.700 --> 01:01:43.300
Thank you very much.

01:01:43.300 --> 01:01:44.300
AMITY SHLAES: Thank you.

01:01:44.300 --> 01:01:46.695
[APPLAUSE]

01:01:53.587 --> 01:01:55.670
MATTHEW SPALDING: I have
so much enjoyed your work

01:01:55.670 --> 01:01:57.830
on your book on Coolidge,
and your work Coolidge

01:01:57.830 --> 01:01:58.900
is wonderful.

01:01:58.900 --> 01:02:01.330
But these two books, The
Forgotten Man and The New Deal,

01:02:01.330 --> 01:02:04.640
if you've not read that,
I really recommend.

01:02:04.640 --> 01:02:07.890
That, and now this new
history of the Great Society,

01:02:07.890 --> 01:02:11.140
will be the contrarian
but very important

01:02:11.140 --> 01:02:13.422
books on liberalism
in the 20th century.

01:02:13.422 --> 01:02:14.380
So thank you very much.

01:02:14.380 --> 01:02:15.470
AMITY SHLAES: Thank you.

01:02:15.470 --> 01:02:17.428
MATTHEW SPALDING: Thank
you all for joining us.

01:02:17.428 --> 01:02:19.410
[APPLAUSE]

